        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "Challenges at Scale",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What are the challenges with using AI at scale?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of reliability engineers considering the challenges that arise when scaling an AI product. They consider error rates, calculate what that error rate might mean in a large user base, and compare the error rate different AI systems can tolerate. Students also write Python code to build linear regression models and evaluate situations in which models might fail to learn useful patterns.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Error Budget Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Print copies of the AI at Scale Unit Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**What are the challenges with using AI at scale?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll take on the role of reliability engineers considering the challenges that arise when scaling an AI product. You'll consider error rates, calculate what that error rate might mean in a large user base, and compare the error rate different AI systems can tolerate."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "Artificial Neurons",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What lessons can we learn from how the brain works?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn about one of the biological bases of learning: the neuron. They explore how artificial neurons model key traits of biological neurons, such as their ability to combine inputs of varying strength and pass the result to other neurons. Students then tinker with their own artificial neurons, using them to solve problems they’ve previously encountered in a new way.\n",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Artificial Neurons Activity Guide for each student.\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**What lessons can we learn from how the brain works?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn about one of the biological bases of learning: the neuron. You'll then explore how artificial neurons model key traits of biological neurons, such as their ability to combine inputs of varying strength and pass the result to other neurons."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-2",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-3",
      "name": "Training Neurons",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can an artificial neuron learn from data?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how an artificial neuron learns from data. Students identify the parameters of a neuron and experiment with those parameters to see how changing them affects the neuron’s behavior. Then, students practice following an algorithm to iteratively update a neuron’s parameters so that it can learn from data.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Training Neurons Activity Guide (one for each student)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can an artificial neuron learn from data?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore how an artificial neuron learns from data. You'll identify the parameters of a neuron and experiment with those parameters to see how changing them affects the neuron’s behavior. Then, you'll practice following an algorithm to iteratively update a neuron’s parameters so that it can learn from data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-3",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-4",
      "name": "Collecting Data for AI",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do you decide if it’s ethical or unethical to use specific types of data for training AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore where AI training data comes from and whether it’s ethical to use. Students evaluate real-world data sources, such as social media posts and smart speaker recordings, through four lenses: consent, privacy, ownership, and bias.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Datasets for AI Activity Guide (one for each pair of student)",
        "student_overview": "**How do you decide if it’s ethical or unethical to use specific types of data for training AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore where AI training data comes from and whether it’s ethical to use. You'll evaluate real-world data sources, such as social media posts and smart speaker recordings, through four lenses: consent, privacy, ownership, and bias.",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-4",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-5",
      "name": "Neural Networks",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can collections of neurons make predictions?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how neural networks are structured and how they can be used to solve regression and classification problems. Students see how artificial neurons can be connected to form artificial neural networks. Then, they look at the weights and biases that make up a neural network and use those values to calculate a neural network’s output. They use those outputs to make predictions and reflect on how the neural network is able to solve problems.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Mars Rover Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can collections of neurons make predictions?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how neural networks are structured and how they can be used to solve regression and classification problems. You'll see how artificial neurons can be connected to form artificial neural networks. Then, you'll look at the weights and biases that make up a neural network and use those values to calculate a neural network’s output.",
        "unplugged": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-5",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-6",
      "name": "Regression with Neural Networks",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can neural networks solve regression problems?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of robotics engineers on a Mars rover team. On paper, they check the engineers’ hand-set speed model against three logged drives and decide which way each parameter should move, as they did by hand in Lesson 6.3. In Python Lab, they train the same network on the drives with gradient descent and watch its predictions converge. Students explain how neurons solve regression problems and train a regression model with gradient descent.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Practice Drives Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n- Pens or pencils\n- Bring students’ completed Training Neurons Activity Guides from Lesson 6.3 (optional, for reference)\n",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can neural networks solve regression problems?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll take on the role of robotics engineers on a Mars rover team. You'll explain how neurons solve regression problems and train a regression model with gradient descent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-6",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-7",
      "name": "Classification with Neural Networks",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can neural networks solve classification problems?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of engineers designing a sorting robot for a recycling facility. On paper, they check a hand-set sorting network against three scanned items and decide which way each weight should move after it sorts one into the wrong bin. In Python Lab, they train the same network on all twelve scanned items with gradient descent and watch its sorting accuracy climb. Students explain how neurons solve classification problems and train a classification model with gradient descent.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Robot Recycling Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can neural networks solve classification problems?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll take on the role of an engineer designing a sorting robot for a recycling facility. You'll explain how neurons solve classification problems and train a classification model with gradient descent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-7",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-8",
      "name": "Safeguards for AI",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we make AI safer to use?**\n\nIn this lesson, students take on the role of a health clinic’s trust and safety team the week before an AI symptom checker launches. On paper, they find the ways the app can be wrong and who each mistake would hurt, then place three kinds of safeguards around it: human review, an audit, and a limit on what the app may do on its own. Students identify possible risks associated with deploying AI systems and design safeguards that protect people from AI errors.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Launch Review Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)",
        "student_overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we make AI safer to use?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll take on the role of a health clinic’s trust and safety team the week before an AI symptom checker launches. You'll identify possible risks associated with deploying AI systems and design safeguards that protect people from AI errors."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-8",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-9",
      "name": "What Neurons Learn",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What are neurons able to learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the limits of what individual neurons are able to learn. Students identify how neurons respond to their inputs, then use that knowledge to consider what kinds of problems neurons can solve and what kinds of problems they can’t solve. Then, students write Python code to train and evaluate neural networks on datasets that don’t follow simple patterns, identifying why their models might fail to learn useful patterns.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Limits of Neurons Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**What are neurons able to learn?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll explore the limits of what individual neurons are able to learn. You'll identify how neurons respond to their inputs, then use that knowledge to consider what kinds of problems neurons can solve and what kinds of problems they can’t solve."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-9",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-10",
      "name": "Deep Learning",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can AI learn complex patterns?**\n\nIn this lesson, students are introduced to deep learning and to neural networks with hidden layers. Students see how hidden layers can allow neural networks to learn smaller patterns that are used to make larger predictions, and they work through the calculations made by a multi-layer neural network. Then, students write Python code to train a multi-layer neural network and compare its performance to a neural network without a hidden layer.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Video Score Activity Guide (one for each pair of students)\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators\n",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI learn complex patterns?**\n\nIn this lesson, you are introduced to deep learning and to neural networks with hidden layers. You'll see how hidden layers can allow neural networks to learn smaller patterns that are used to make larger predictions, and you'll work through the calculations made by a multi-layer neural network. Then, you'll write Python code to train a multi-layer neural network and compare its performance to a neural network without a hidden layer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-10",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-11",
      "name": "Hardware for AI",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What do computers need to run AI efficiently?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how computers can efficiently process large neural networks. They begin the lesson by discovering that parallel processing—the ability to perform many computations at once—significantly improves the speed at which the output of a neural network can be computed. Students then learn to distinguish CPUs and GPUs, comparing how efficiently each runs a neural network.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Processing Neural Networks Activity Guide for each pair of students.\n- Print and cut out one copy of the Processing Neural Networks Cards sheet for every five pairs of students. Set aside each set of six cards separately.",
        "student_overview": "**What do computers need to run AI efficiently?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how computers can efficiently process large neural networks."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-11",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-12",
      "name": "Costs of AI",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What resources are needed to run AI at scale?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how to measure computational work and evaluate the costs—monetary, environmental, and social—of that work. They first learn about deep learning recommendation models (DLRMs), a large neural network-based model that requires significant computing power. Students estimate the computational work required to run such a model and use their estimate to infer hardware and power requirements. They then explore a sandbox where they tinker with a model’s deployment requirements, such as the number of concurrent requests it needs to service, and evaluate tradeoffs between performance and cost. Finally, students consider the data centers that house the hardware to power AI models like DLRMs, naming the many costs of these buildings.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Costs of Computation Activity Guide\n- Provide, or students to bring, blank sheets of paper\n- Provide, or ask students to bring, calculators",
        "student_overview": "**What resources are needed to run AI at scale?**\n\nIn this lesson, you'll learn how to measure computational work and evaluate the costs—monetary, environmental, and social—of that work."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-12",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-13",
      "name": "Neural Network Project",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "In this lesson, students use neural networks to build an AI program that helps their community. Students choose a problem of interest to them, select a dataset that’s a good fit for their work, and train a neural network to make predictions. They also look at user feedback and concerns to determine how their neural network will be used to make decisions and what safeguards they’ll put in place. Finally, students prepare a digital poster where they showcase their work and its potential value to the community.",
        "preparation": "- Print one copy of the Neural Network Project Planning Guide for each student. ",
        "student_overview": "In this lesson, you'll use neural networks to build an AI program that helps your community. You'll choose a problem of interest to you, select a dataset that’s a good fit for your work, and train a neural network to make predictions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-13",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-year2-semester1-unit6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-15",
