  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "ee4e5cb7-6057-4eb7-9bba-1fe16a5b1e0c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee4e5cb7-6057-4eb7-9bba-1fe16a5b1e0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5248fcb3-6dcf-4f05-b663-99a94b85a43d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f79e394-1588-4cd8-ab2a-4aebee1179d3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Every morning, millions of people open a weather app before they leave home. The app’s rain alert comes from a machine learning model. The model is right 97 times out of 100.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f79e394-1588-4cd8-ab2a-4aebee1179d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8d78219-d330-41d8-b624-ea495154fb77"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d8e7040b-6ea7-4260-9265-ec327d769aa6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is a weather app that correctly predicts rain 97 times out of 100 good enough? What would change your mind?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students take a position on whether a model is good enough and justify their reasoning. Strong responses will make a claim and consider real-world use cases to argue for their position. For example, students might say that three errors sounds like a small number, so most of the time people will get correct predictions about weather that isn’t always possible to predict perfectly. Encourage students to think about what circumstances might change their answer. Factors that might affect whether the model is good enough include: (a) who the users are, (b) how many users there are, and (c) what the cost is for a wrong rain alert. Help students see that the same error rate can have different impacts depending on the circumstances.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d8e7040b-6ea7-4260-9265-ec327d769aa6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8d78219-d330-41d8-b624-ea495154fb77"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "65bd9739-f296-4f44-8649-ed6aec607341",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As AI gets used by more people, we always need to keep in mind its reliability and impact.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "65bd9739-f296-4f44-8649-ed6aec607341",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8d78219-d330-41d8-b624-ea495154fb77"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a21ed76-482a-47d2-98e1-43700e0e80af",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a reliability engineer.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a21ed76-482a-47d2-98e1-43700e0e80af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8d78219-d330-41d8-b624-ea495154fb77"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a2666d16-e733-4330-a10f-7ea86c2d2c86",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will be playing the role of reliability engineers at a software company that publishes and maintains a weather app. A reliability engineer maintains systems that serve many people. They measure how often a system fails, decide how much failure is acceptable, and fix what breaks at scale. Reliability engineers work at any company whose software has to work for everyone at once: maps, messaging, banking, streaming, and the apps on your phone.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a2666d16-e733-4330-a10f-7ea86c2d2c86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8d78219-d330-41d8-b624-ea495154fb77"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b2f1b13-de05-4740-87ee-eed71d1b9b60",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b2f1b13-de05-4740-87ee-eed71d1b9b60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f8d78219-d330-41d8-b624-ea495154fb77"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae016acc-6067-41ae-a74a-ab32ef8283fc",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students work out what a 3 percent error rate means at four different scales, from a grade level to sixty million users. They then compare three AI systems by what a wrong answer costs and the error rate each can tolerate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _scale_.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "The Error Budget"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae016acc-6067-41ae-a74a-ab32ef8283fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58fd8077-62c5-40b3-a201-8ec3a98d8d72",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A popular weather app can operate at a large scale. It learns from millions of readings, it makes forecasts for every town in the country, and millions of people might check it before leaving home. Today, we’ll focus on the people that are affected by AI at scale.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students were introduced to a different use of the term “scale” in Unit 4 with feature scaling, which resizes numbers so they use a standardized numeric range. If students ask, say that both uses are about size, but this lesson’s use of scale is about the size of an AI system, not the size of the numbers in its dataset.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58fd8077-62c5-40b3-a201-8ec3a98d8d72",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6cd3a04-30ff-4c63-95e9-d90d1b169c53",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The app’s rain alert is right 97 times out of 100. As the reliability team, your job is to work out what the error rate means at the scale of the whole app and to tell the team what you found. Your users are spread across many towns, so count an average day.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6cd3a04-30ff-4c63-95e9-d90d1b169c53",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15ca644f-3aaf-439c-aa52-5bcd59fd5ca2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Error Budget Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete both pages of the activity guide with their partner. Each student writes on their own copy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Some students may be unsure how to extrapolate error rates to a larger group. One approach is to think in units of 100: if there are 3 errors in every 100 users, then for ten times as many users, we might expect 30 errors for 1,000 users. Students could also multiply the number of users by 0.03 (the 3% expressed in decimal form).\n- Some students might not explain their reasoning for why an error rate could be a problem. Encourage students to justify their answers with numbers: they can use their responses in the table to reason about why even a small error rate could be a problem for a large population.\n- Students might pick the same error rate for all three systems on page 2. Ask what one wrong answer costs in each row, and whether the person who pays that cost would accept the rate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which of the three systems should never run at 3 wrong in 100? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students compare the three systems by what a wrong answer costs, not by the rate alone, as they share their answers with the class. Strong responses say that the self-driving car’s pedestrian detector cannot run at 3 in 100 because one wrong answer can injure someone, and that at the scale of every car on the road even 1 in 10,000 reaches many people, while a wrong rain alert costs a wet walk. Help students see that the acceptable error rate depends on the cost of each mistake and on how many people the system serves."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15ca644f-3aaf-439c-aa52-5bcd59fd5ca2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "046b7926-3210-4afe-8aed-8f7bd8bc8282",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The rate never changed in your table. What changed was the number of people, and three in a hundred became 1.8 million a day. Deciding how many and what kinds of errors a system can make is part of a reliability engineer’s job.\n\nReliability engineers don’t just look at how many mistakes are made. They also look at the data to understand what kinds of mistakes happen and why. That’s what we’ll explore next.\n\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "046b7926-3210-4afe-8aed-8f7bd8bc8282",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b4b70a21-9ba7-472d-8749-ae4b7e40c26e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students fit a simple straight-line linear regression model to weather readings and observe how it fails based on the shape of the data.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Testing a Weather Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b4b70a21-9ba7-472d-8749-ae4b7e40c26e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "271b837c-42bc-47e6-a9ab-1dd3b91425d3",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Our weather app does more than send alerts. It also fills gaps in the day’s readings with a model. The team wants to test the linear regression model that fits a straight line to the data. You have one day of readings from one weather station, one reading per hour. Train the model to predict the 3 pm and 11 pm readings, and look at the chart.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "271b837c-42bc-47e6-a9ab-1dd3b91425d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3582825-e415-4e8d-a717-9cb00f61e067",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the eight lines of code. Lines 1 to 5 are given: the import, the hours, each wrapped in its own list, and the day’s temperatures. Line 6 creates the untrained model. Lines 7 and 8 are the two TODOs: line 7 trains the model, and line 8 asks it for the 3 pm temperature; the next line of the starter code prints that prediction beside the actual reading.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask the class why line 8 says 15 for 3 pm. The hours run from 0 at midnight to 23, so 3 pm is hour 15. Then ask why 15 sits in double brackets. Then, explain."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3582825-e415-4e8d-a717-9cb00f61e067",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0692f3a2-50f5-427d-b301-118753ab2d03",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Every input to scikit-learn is wrapped in its own list, so one hour is `[[15]]`, the same shape as the hours list on line 2, where every hour already sits in its own list. The prediction comes back as a list too, which is what the `[0]` opens. The Coding Tip on the level shows both lines again.\n\nYour starter is this same program with the two TODO lines left for you, plus a helper import at the top, a print after each prediction, and one given line at the end that draws the chart. The slide shows the 3 pm prediction; you also write the same lines for 11 pm.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0692f3a2-50f5-427d-b301-118753ab2d03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a1eb473-f9cd-444d-871f-c58c02da5ab9",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Testing Models"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a1eb473-f9cd-444d-871f-c58c02da5ab9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f464a9f3-9dec-4df0-84a9-876a83f6fae4",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f464a9f3-9dec-4df0-84a9-876a83f6fae4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e417cea-3c79-4668-b125-666a51480bea",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Levels 1-4.\n- Level 1: Students train a linear regression model on a day of hourly temperatures and interpret the results.\n- Level 2: Students create and train the model on a day of humidity readings.\n- Level 3: Students import the class, create the model and train it on a day of UV index readings.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check Your Understanding on the mistakes the model made and why the data did not fit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students might see only dots on the chart. The line appears once the model is trained, so point them to the `model.fit` call from Lesson 4.1, with hours first and the readings second.\n- Students might see a message that the model is not fitted. They are predicting before training, so the training line comes before the prediction.\n- Students might predict with a plain number instead of a list inside a list. Remind them that `scikit-learn` expects every input wrapped in its own list, so the 3 pm prediction is `model.predict([[15]])[0]`.\n- Students might not remember how to create the model in Level 2 or the import in Level 3, and the program stops at the chart line with a `NameError` until they do. Point them to the starter of the level before, where the line was given.\n- If students finish early, ask them to find the hours where the line is almost right and to say in words why a line can be right at some hours and wrong at others.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students can describe a kind of mistake the model made and explain why the data did not fit it. Strong responses (a) name a specific miss, such as the line predicting 82 degrees at 11 pm when the reading was 65, (b) explain that a straight line cannot follow readings that rise and then fall, and (c) mention unrepresentative data or too little data. If students struggle, return to the chart and ask where the line is too high or too low.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e417cea-3c79-4668-b125-666a51480bea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50bc26fc-967d-446b-bfe7-4fa393801dca",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show an example of the temperature chart with a linear regression line drawn over the data.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50bc26fc-967d-446b-bfe7-4fa393801dca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4033a43a-a069-4692-85b9-3e9dc98d7aaf",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The line doesn’t do a very good job of predicting: the line predicts a temperature that’s too cold at 3 pm and a temperature that’s too warm at 11 pm. But there’s no better line that we can draw: a line just isn’t a good fit for the shape of this data, so a linear regression model isn’t going to be able to learn a useful pattern. In this unit, we’ll start to explore other models that can.\n\nYou have just seen one way a model might not learn a pattern: when the data is the wrong shape for the model. Another possibility that you’ve seen is that the data isn’t representative: a model trained on one city’s weather patterns might not do a good job of understanding patterns in weather in other cities. And yet another possibility is that there is too little data to demonstrate the pattern: a model needs enough data to be able to learn a pattern.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4033a43a-a069-4692-85b9-3e9dc98d7aaf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c96e5e4-49c1-4a3a-ad91-fbf188d7bdf4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a0febae6-e292-40ee-9cfd-84cad2608cab",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a0febae6-e292-40ee-9cfd-84cad2608cab",
        "lesson_activity.key": "07309122-e241-4b45-ad95-6b0a72fbf84a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8df47ac4-6a31-419a-886b-556e150136d9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce handwriting recognition as an example of a problem that becomes difficult at scale, but that is solvable by taking inspiration from the human brain.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the task of handwriting recognition, calling back to the previous lesson on problems that become challenging at scale.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Handwriting Recognition"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8df47ac4-6a31-419a-886b-556e150136d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "474cc414-db3c-4424-8dbb-d08673063fb0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If you’ve ever tried to read messy handwriting—or any handwriting at all!—you’ve encountered the problem of handwriting recognition. This is a good example of a problem that becomes difficult for computers at scale. It’s one thing to read one person’s handwriting. But it’s another to read everyone’s handwriting. There are effectively as many handwriting styles as there are people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "474cc414-db3c-4424-8dbb-d08673063fb0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4626ae5-f872-482b-bf55-bf074c9e7973",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "And yet, while reading handwriting can be challenging for a computer, it’s not quite as difficult for humans. In most cases, if we try hard enough, we can identify what someone has written. Let’s try it!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4626ae5-f872-482b-bf55-bf074c9e7973",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22b35d00-54f9-4053-ba1f-d5a4366364d7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to guess what this handwriting represents."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22b35d00-54f9-4053-ba1f-d5a4366364d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "47375701-1056-478d-8116-5951f2201616",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What did this person write?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "47375701-1056-478d-8116-5951f2201616",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff723943-abc6-47e1-ad40-758970f0bbd2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Collect guesses from students in a way that suits your classroom. Reveal that the person wrote the number 2.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optionally seek out students who see something different. For example, a plausible interpretation is that the picture is a curly, lowercase letter L. Take disagreement as an opportunity to highlight the ambiguous nature of handwriting recognition problems."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff723943-abc6-47e1-ad40-758970f0bbd2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a6805b6-093f-4899-85fa-e66004997ef1",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Again prompt students to make a guess about what this handwriting represents. Collect responses in a way that suits your classroom. Then, reveal that the handwriting represents the number 7.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once again prompt students to make a guess about what this handwriting represents. Collect responses in a way that suits your classroom. Then, reveal that the handwriting also represents the number 7."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a6805b6-093f-4899-85fa-e66004997ef1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3df90a1f-451b-437b-811f-08f357a9f4f9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here we see two different ways to write the number 7. That’s another reason handwriting recognition is difficult. Not only does everyone have a different handwriting style, some numbers and letters have multiple valid ways to write them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3df90a1f-451b-437b-811f-08f357a9f4f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d27d8215-0e9b-4446-bb4b-dc7cb250ab3f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We know that humans—and more specifically, the human brain—are generally good at reading handwriting. If we wanted to make a computer program that is also good at reading handwriting, is there any inspiration we could take from the brain?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d27d8215-0e9b-4446-bb4b-dc7cb250ab3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3744154f-7503-41ee-97e4-edaa8bfa9252"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76296986-6159-4249-aecf-4bd551ad53c5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Suppose an AI engineer wants to build a handwriting recognition system. What inspiration do you think they could take from the human brain?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their intuitions about what makes humans—and more specifically, the human brain—good at handwriting recognition, as well as what features of the brain could be simulated to help a computer program accomplish the same task. Strong responses will identify a strength of the brain and draw a connection to something a computer program could do. For example, students might suggest that the brain learns how to recognize handwriting by seeing lots of examples, so a computer program could learn from lots of examples too. They may also suggest that the brain is highly flexible, so a good goal might be to make a computer program that is similarly flexible. There are no right or wrong answers: the primary goal is to surface what students know about the brain and what makes it intelligent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76296986-6159-4249-aecf-4bd551ad53c5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5bca33c6-fa8d-4f00-a250-a2f3f1cecb2c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab7a1cfa-75d4-4294-831c-c0cb2229868d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we’ll explore what inspiration AI can take from the human brain, towards the goal of writing even more intelligent programs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab7a1cfa-75d4-4294-831c-c0cb2229868d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24dfe494-ed6b-43de-ae30-5aefad132810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "be33a003-39aa-44df-bf02-f408b49c41d5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "be33a003-39aa-44df-bf02-f408b49c41d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "24dfe494-ed6b-43de-ae30-5aefad132810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3bf7746-7289-423a-bbb9-1eb7e862608e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to neurons, cells involved in learning, and to artificial neurons, a model that takes inspiration from the role of neurons in learning.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Introducing Artificial Neurons"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3bf7746-7289-423a-bbb9-1eb7e862608e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3435795e-77c9-44fc-806f-e05aa846fb15",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the things that makes the human brain so intelligent is its ability to learn. One of the cells involved in learning is the neuron.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3435795e-77c9-44fc-806f-e05aa846fb15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b30e488-69d7-48ab-a8e4-e7fbf539bb92",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *neuron*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b30e488-69d7-48ab-a8e4-e7fbf539bb92",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bca3450a-501b-4a8d-818c-7828ec17b072",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The human brain is composed of many, many billions of neurons, connected in incredible ways. These connections help you learn and behave intelligently.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If of interest to students, share that many animals are capable of learning too. Their brains, like human brains, have neurons that grow, form connections, and communicate. Neurons are not unique to the human brain."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bca3450a-501b-4a8d-818c-7828ec17b072",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e895923-5f7e-446e-b2de-383ef0b2cecc",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Neurons Connecting\" video (26 seconds)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e895923-5f7e-446e-b2de-383ef0b2cecc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5de5c6ad-b13a-4bbe-97c9-975d28becf5c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this sped-up video, we can see a neuron in a lab dish growing and forming a connection with another neuron. Your brain did this billions of times as it developed, and even now, the connections between your neurons keep changing in small ways as you learn.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If you have time, consider prompting students to share their reactions in a short class discussion. Ask: \"What do you notice? What do you wonder?\" There are no right or wrong answers."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5de5c6ad-b13a-4bbe-97c9-975d28becf5c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e9ffd61-890c-4dc3-891f-229d199c0c08",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Since neurons are so connected to the process of learning, AI engineers thought that modeling them in a computer might create more intelligent programs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e9ffd61-890c-4dc3-891f-229d199c0c08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52322b81-d373-43a8-a255-20aa7345d993",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *artificial neuron*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52322b81-d373-43a8-a255-20aa7345d993",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "53925c9c-ddee-4c3a-852d-474652ad1b59",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll get to tinker with an artificial neuron in just a moment. Before that, let’s get familiar with their key traits.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "53925c9c-ddee-4c3a-852d-474652ad1b59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23001788-30b3-4a00-9964-0eec38f01633",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Illustrate the key traits of a biological neuron that an artificial neuron models.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As you teach, optionally illustrate these ideas in the Artificial Neuron Widget. Each idea is represented by an element of the widget: the weighted inputs, the weighted sum, and the labeled \"Output\"."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23001788-30b3-4a00-9964-0eec38f01633",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "daafdd68-a712-4f43-89a2-f472af11728e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "An artificial neuron models a few key traits of a biological neuron.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "daafdd68-a712-4f43-89a2-f472af11728e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1ea7b01-13a1-476a-abe7-d1d558400101",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "First, a biological neuron receives signals from other neurons, and some signals are stronger than others. An artificial neuron receives numbers instead, and each number has a weight that tells the neuron how much it should be influenced by the number. A larger weight gives that number more influence.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1ea7b01-13a1-476a-abe7-d1d558400101",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dabe3c89-12c0-47ce-a20e-9d38c4bf7959",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Next, a biological neuron combines all the signals it receives to determine the strength of its own signal. A stronger combined signal makes it fire electrical pulses more often. An artificial neuron multiplies each number by its weight, adds them up, adds a bias, and uses the total to calculate its output.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dabe3c89-12c0-47ce-a20e-9d38c4bf7959",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9364946e-2037-4674-b34c-dbe5f3510bb3",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Finally, a biological neuron sends its signal on to other neurons, and an artificial neuron sends its output on to other neurons. In both cases, one neuron's output becomes the next neuron's input.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9364946e-2037-4674-b34c-dbe5f3510bb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b1cacaa-3622-4714-8730-d6b6ad80438b",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the representation of an artificial neuron students will engage with."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b1cacaa-3622-4714-8730-d6b6ad80438b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04975ab3-f141-45ad-a92d-6a444fcd2a9d",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s a representation of an artificial neuron. It takes two inputs, each with their own weights. It also has a bias—an amount that’s always added. Let’s walk through how this neuron’s inputs and bias give us the output we see.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04975ab3-f141-45ad-a92d-6a444fcd2a9d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5b1929b-980b-4f13-82f4-b1a02472652b",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Step through the calculation, mapping its components to the picture of the artificial neuron."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5b1929b-980b-4f13-82f4-b1a02472652b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76ec0cb8-edef-47c3-90a2-c49fbf24b0c4",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Provide an opportunity for students to learn about artificial neurons, and how they solve problems, by experimenting with the Artificial Neuron Widget.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Tinkering with Artificial Neurons"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76ec0cb8-edef-47c3-90a2-c49fbf24b0c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "052c733c-ad4b-4a85-acea-917e39efefe0",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the best ways to understand artificial neurons is to play with them—which is what you’ll do now. You’ll explore how a single artificial neuron can help you solve some of the problems you’ve already seen in this course: from estimating how much a ride share will cost, to estimating how many students will attend a musical.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "052c733c-ad4b-4a85-acea-917e39efefe0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa2bbefb-8da6-426d-8fe5-f245e03d1d43",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Artificial Neurons Activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa2bbefb-8da6-426d-8fe5-f245e03d1d43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e58f986f-cd86-47ed-9862-38c123531dc8",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you tinker with artificial neurons, you’ll follow along with their calculations in your Activity Guide.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e58f986f-cd86-47ed-9862-38c123531dc8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a224d83-06b1-4195-9105-2847f6cc1d4f",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Artificial Neurons"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a224d83-06b1-4195-9105-2847f6cc1d4f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7f3a988-ccee-4106-8455-4fd2475fcd24",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7f3a988-ccee-4106-8455-4fd2475fcd24",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e8cdfbb-f2a0-4d7f-a597-0da4139461c1",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Levels 1–4.\n- **Level 1:** Students experiment with an artificial neuron that has one weighted input to solve a problem of their choice.\n- **Level 2:** Students experiment with an artificial neuron that has two weighted inputs to solve a problem of their choice. They see that some inputs can result in a neuron with a negative output.\n- **Level 3:** Students learn about the ReLU—Rectified Linear Unit—function, which can address situations where a neuron should not have a negative output.\n- **Level 4:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who aren’t sure what their neuron’s weights or bias should be, based on the problem’s description. Guide them in reviewing the definition of a weight and a bias, pointing out which parts of the problem correspond to which definition.\n- Students who move quickly through the activity, without pausing to explore various inputs. Remind them they’ll be asked to share their understanding of artificial neurons in the final level, so it’s helpful for them to spend time exploring and building their understanding.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 as an assessment of how well students have understood the lesson’s objectives. Strong responses will identify a few traits of a biological neuron that an artificial neuron models, and correctly calculate the value of an artificial neuron given specific inputs."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e8cdfbb-f2a0-4d7f-a597-0da4139461c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dba7b650-3510-4591-b6d4-8aced4d07c04",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Once students have made it past Level 3, define *activation function*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dba7b650-3510-4591-b6d4-8aced4d07c04",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b03b0a3f-7ec4-47f7-b5d6-3891988089b5",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve already seen an example of an activation function. In Level 3, you applied the ReLU activation function: this function ensures that a neuron’s output will never be less than 0.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b03b0a3f-7ec4-47f7-b5d6-3891988089b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e832966-6379-45ac-9d05-7649c803ae6d",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students that other activation functions, besides ReLU, exist. Explain that the choice of activation function can influence how a neuron behaves. Briefly show students other activation functions—though emphasize they don’t need to fully understand the mathematics behind them.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How is a one-input neuron like a linear regression model?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to compare a one-input neuron, visually and mathematically, to a linear regression model. Strong responses will make a connection between the neuron’s bias and a linear regression model’s y-intercept, as well as a connection between a neuron’s weight and the linear regression model’s slope. Guide the discussion towards the idea that a single-input neuron ultimately is a linear function, the same as a linear regression model.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How is a multi-input neuron like a multiple linear regression model?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to compare a multi-input neuron, visually and mathematically, to a multiple linear regression model. Strong responses will make a connection between the neuron’s bias and a linear regression model’s y-intercept, as well as a connection between a neuron’s weights and the multiple linear regression model’s weights. Guide the discussion towards the idea that a multi-input neuron ultimately is a linear function with multiple variables, the same as a multiple linear regression model, and that a two-input neuron’s output could be viewed as a 2D plane in 3D space.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Preview upcoming lessons on neural networks."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e832966-6379-45ac-9d05-7649c803ae6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2dafe5d0-cf80-4b86-af86-99f07c43a338",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve seen that a single neuron is essentially a linear regression model. A single neuron, then, can only solve the same problems as linear regression. In the rest of this unit, we’ll consider what networks of neurons can learn that linear regression models cannot.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2dafe5d0-cf80-4b86-af86-99f07c43a338",
        "lesson_activity.key": "573cd249-8334-4f2b-be52-3ecf044602c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2277e3c8-65da-44a0-b009-5b7dce9bdcdb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2277e3c8-65da-44a0-b009-5b7dce9bdcdb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "72b41e3b-56d3-4b0d-a3ff-b5da22b143f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da5ad3a1-bfea-44a8-9b94-9210c4a6cc11",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Think of a number from 1 to 100, but don’t tell your students. A good choice of value would be one that isn’t a likely first guess and isn’t too close to either extreme (for example, 28).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to try to guess your number. Call on one student a time: let the student make a guess, then reply with “Correct!”, “Your guess is too high” or “Your guess is too low.” Continue calling on students until students successfully guess your number. If students make optimal guesses, it should take no more than seven guesses, but it may take a few more depending on how students choose to guess.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get particularly lucky and guess the number in three or fewer tries, consider picking a different number and trying again. This gives students more opportunity to consider their guessing strategy in preparation for the next discussion.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da5ad3a1-bfea-44a8-9b94-9210c4a6cc11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59d6dff-55c4-40e2-aca7-d1a3aa70be34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85adc8c0-6aa7-481d-8ab5-32f8f232150e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What strategy did you use to make each guess? How did the quality of your guesses change over time?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on their guessing process and recognize several characteristics of it. First, students should recognize that initially, they lacked information: they could make a random guess, but it wasn’t likely to be right. Students should also identify that as they got more information, that helped them narrow down the range of possible numbers: their initial guesses were likely very different from each other, but over time the guesses became more similar as students became more confident about a narrower range of correct values. Students might note that their guess quality increased with time: the more they guessed, the more information they got, and the more they could refine their guesses to be more accurate. Help students see that while they started with a random guess, as they got more information, they were able to focus in on the right range of numbers and make slight adjustments until they got the right answer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85adc8c0-6aa7-481d-8ab5-32f8f232150e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59d6dff-55c4-40e2-aca7-d1a3aa70be34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "424145c2-cfe9-4373-8aeb-31fe90b28e5e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Artificial neurons use a similar process when they’re learning. They need to learn what weights and biases will help them make good predictions, but at first they don’t know what the right value should be. But by learning from data, they can adjust their weights and biases until they close in on a good answer.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "424145c2-cfe9-4373-8aeb-31fe90b28e5e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59d6dff-55c4-40e2-aca7-d1a3aa70be34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b14a73a1-0f8d-41b6-b4e0-542f4b64338f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b14a73a1-0f8d-41b6-b4e0-542f4b64338f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d59d6dff-55c4-40e2-aca7-d1a3aa70be34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31656943-a70c-45f7-8b5a-df6a521bb179",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students get hands-on practice changing weights and biases to construct neurons that can perform particular calculations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students of the structure of an artificial neuron. Artificial neurons have inputs, weights, and biases. When an artificial neuron receives inputs, it multiplies each input by its corresponding weight and adds the bias to get a weighted sum. If students need additional help remembering how artificial neurons work, walk through the sample calculation on the slide: the neuron calculates (1)(3) + (0)(4) + 5 = 8.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out how weights and biases differ from inputs. The inputs are provided to the neuron, whereas the weights and bias are values that the neuron must learn.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Experimenting with Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31656943-a70c-45f7-8b5a-df6a521bb179",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c4e0dc0-368f-4651-a581-914519f6f238",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The weights and bias of a neuron are how it calculates its output from the input. These are values that the neuron needs to learn through data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c4e0dc0-368f-4651-a581-914519f6f238",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d994fb9c-fe9b-4683-9ee2-f5f90c3dbd69",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Check students’ understanding by asking them how many values the neuron in this example needs to learn. Help students see that it needs to learn three: two weights and one bias.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Often, artificial neurons will also have activation functions that are applied after the weighted sum. For simplicity, this lesson focuses on artificial neurons without activation functions.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d994fb9c-fe9b-4683-9ee2-f5f90c3dbd69",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8fbbd16b-7206-49d8-a13d-fc609238c600",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _parameter_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8fbbd16b-7206-49d8-a13d-fc609238c600",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1dfc58c5-dbb1-44da-b4d5-b5cdc7d5b52d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A neuron’s parameters are its weights and its bias. When training a neuron, the neuron learns what weights and bias values to use to make good predictions. Once it’s learned the right weights and bias values, it can use those values on new inputs.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1dfc58c5-dbb1-44da-b4d5-b5cdc7d5b52d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c7474b3-225f-40f1-9674-4e91dd505a1d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute a copy of the Training Neurons Activity Guide to each student.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c7474b3-225f-40f1-9674-4e91dd505a1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d56d3a3-e16e-4191-aaf2-817e9a3a7986",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ll use this activity guide as you complete the levels in today’s lesson.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d56d3a3-e16e-4191-aaf2-817e9a3a7986",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "734a72b9-cbac-495b-a950-82f20026131d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Experiment with Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "734a72b9-cbac-495b-a950-82f20026131d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4e5311e-34f3-4739-a172-510f8579b9a3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Levels 1–2.\n- Level 1: Students experiment with a neuron’s parameters so that it can make a single prediction successfully.\n- Level 2: Students experiment with a neuron’s parameters so that it can make three predictions successfully.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who don’t know where to begin with Level 1. Remind students how to calculate the weighted sum for an artificial neuron: it’s the result of multiplying each input by its weight and adding the bias. In this lesson, all inputs are binary (0s or 1s), which means the formula could be described more simply as the sum of all weights corresponding to inputs of 1 (ignoring weights corresponding to inputs of 0), plus the bias. Help students use this formula to find weights that work: one simple option is to use the training example’s output as a weight. That said, there are many possible solutions that work: encourage students to tinker with the parameters, read the resulting calculation, and iterate until they find something that works.\n- Students who struggle with the multiple training examples in Level 2. Level 2 intentionally doesn’t specify a method students should use for finding parameters, and there are multiple approaches students could use: some might work through one input at a time and try to modify parameters in such a way that the outputs work out, others might look at similar pairs of inputs and infer what weights should be, others might write down the equations for neuron output and solve for the parameters mathematically. Any of these approaches can work. If students get stuck, encourage students to collaborate with each other, or try to get them to get the neuron working for just one or two inputs first and then try the third.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What strategies did you use to learn the right values for the parameters?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students talk through the approach they used for finding parameter values. There isn’t a single right answer: the point here is to surface a mix of both precise and imprecise ways to train a neuron. Strong responses will describe a strategy, even if the strategy isn’t algorithmically precise. For example, students might describe how they tried an initial set of weights and adjusted them up or down if they didn’t work. Encourage students to describe how they handled the more challenging case where there were multiple training examples: students may describe that they found parameters that worked well with one example, but then needed to adjust the parameters so that they worked with the other training examples."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4e5311e-34f3-4739-a172-510f8579b9a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e555fa64-e50a-4e15-8f90-355b9c03d9ac",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The more parameters and the more data a neuron has to work with, the more work it takes to find parameters that will do a good job across all the data. Often, we won’t get the parameters right the first time, but we can look at how the neuron behaves and decide how to adjust our parameters so they get closer to the right values.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e555fa64-e50a-4e15-8f90-355b9c03d9ac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed9d3349-0432-4b8a-97c6-200e9ee5084a",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore how weights can be adjusted in response to training examples. They use an algorithm to iteratively update parameters of a neuron so that the neuron’s performance improves over time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Present students with an example of a neuron. It computes a predicted output of 8, but the actual output (according to training data) was supposed to be a 10.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Learning Parameters Algorithmically"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed9d3349-0432-4b8a-97c6-200e9ee5084a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c16398b4-c72a-46f5-818b-2b968bb4bdda",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This neuron predicted the wrong output, so we should adjust the parameters so the neuron does a better job. Are the parameters too big or too small?\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c16398b4-c72a-46f5-818b-2b968bb4bdda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ee8bbc2c-1c2b-432c-9d69-b12741b98bd0",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Take suggestions from students. Help students see that the parameters in this case are too small: when the predicted output is smaller than the actual output, we need to make the predicted output larger. Increasing the weights and bias can help achieve that.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that even once we know the parameters need to increase, we need to decide by how much they need to increase. One idea might be to look at the actual output (10) and the predicted output (8). Since the values are 2 apart, a possibility would be to increase each parameter by 2. (This approach gets at the idea that the more wrong the prediction is, the more the parameters need to change. This strategy doesn’t quite work though—which will be explored on the next slide.)",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The conclusion students reach here—that the parameters should increase when the predicted output is less than the actual output—holds in this case because the inputs are positive. If students are feeling comfortable with the material, and if time permits, consider inviting students to think about what would happen if the inputs were negative instead.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ee8bbc2c-1c2b-432c-9d69-b12741b98bd0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0380c7ee-4685-49bf-bbe5-cfe098e248ad",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve increased the parameters, but the neuron still predicts the wrong output. Are these parameters too big or too small?\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0380c7ee-4685-49bf-bbe5-cfe098e248ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "039de9da-bd72-4e17-b59e-4641645f8796",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Take suggestions from students. Help students see that the parameters in this case are now too big: when the predicted output is larger than the actual output, we need to make the predicted output smaller. Decreasing the weights and bias can help achieve that.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "039de9da-bd72-4e17-b59e-4641645f8796",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9ff1cfd-ba4c-4f5d-b98c-7a70be0cc9f5",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When we adjust parameters to make a neuron more accurate, we have to decide how much to adjust them. If we adjust them too little, the neuron’s accuracy might not improve enough. If we adjust them too much, the neuron might become less accurate.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9ff1cfd-ba4c-4f5d-b98c-7a70be0cc9f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f843d1f-3a78-4630-a0b8-d99c7596e788",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _learning rate_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f843d1f-3a78-4630-a0b8-d99c7596e788",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a03c8f2-66b8-4267-9d56-179fd0543ec3",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The learning rate is a value that machine learning engineers choose when training neurons. The learning rate determines how fast parameters change: training a neuron works best when the learning rate isn’t too fast or too slow.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a03c8f2-66b8-4267-9d56-179fd0543ec3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7f3cac4-de20-4ee7-854c-a4982c9771d3",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce one more scenario that gets students thinking about which parameters need to change, not just in what direction they need to change. Show a neuron where one input is 1 and the other input is 0. The neuron predicts the wrong output. Ask students to think about which parameters need to be changed so that the neuron is more accurate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Take suggestions from students. Help students see that Weight 2 doesn’t influence the neuron’s calculation at all, because Input 2 is 0. The lesson students should take from this is that the only parameters that affect the neuron’s output are the weights for inputs of 1 and the bias. Weights for inputs of 0 have no effect. As a result, the parameters that should change in this case are Weight 1 and the bias."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7f3cac4-de20-4ee7-854c-a4982c9771d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37987a8a-d7d7-4bd4-97e5-df728bd20a89",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We now have a few principles we can use for training neurons. Each time we look at a training example, we want to adjust some parameters, but not adjust them too much. And we should only adjust the parameters that were actually used when calculating the neuron’s output. You’ll use these ideas in the next activity.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In this lesson, students follow the rule that weights should change for inputs of 1 and weights don’t need to change for inputs of 0. This rule is sufficient for this lesson because all inputs students work with are 0s or 1s. In general, inputs to neurons can also take on other values. If students are feeling comfortable with the material, you can ask them to think about how they would change parameters if both inputs are positive, but one is larger than the other. Help students recognize that the larger input has more of an effect on the neuron’s output, so its weight should change more.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37987a8a-d7d7-4bd4-97e5-df728bd20a89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da1c4847-6843-4f6b-81ab-8f63bc2830a5",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Iteratively Update Parameters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da1c4847-6843-4f6b-81ab-8f63bc2830a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c525089-0fd4-4dc4-8a51-c018b6c6fa7b",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c525089-0fd4-4dc4-8a51-c018b6c6fa7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a2d2189-518a-4537-82bb-e7bf0f850697",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Levels 3–4.\n- Level 3: Students follow an algorithm to iteratively update the parameters of an artificial neuron, making the neuron more accurate over time.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check Your Understanding to reflect on why their update process resulted in making the neuron more accurate.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who are unsure what the table in the activity guide represents. Explain to students that the table represents the process of training the neuron to learn what parameters to use. Initially, in the first row of the table, all parameters are 0. Students will work one row at a time: each row uses a specific example from the training data to determine how to adjust the parameters. Students write the updated parameter values into that row, and then use those updated values when moving on to the next row.\n- Students who reuse the initial parameters (all 0s) for each iteration of the algorithm. Each iteration of the algorithm produces an updated set of parameters, and students should use those updated parameters on the next iteration of the algorithm. Help students see how this process results in adjusting weights to produce a more accurate neuron a small step at a time.\n- Students who have trouble knowing how much to adjust parameters. Work with students to use the formula in their activity guide. The formula computes the adjustment as half the difference between the actual value and the predicted value. This value is a value that should be added to a previous parameter value: it might not be a whole number, and it might not be positive.\n- Students who have trouble knowing which parameters to adjust. The bias is used in every calculation, so the bias should always be adjusted. Weights for inputs of values of 1 were used in the calculation, so those weights should be adjusted. Weights for inputs of values of 0 were not used in the calculation, so those weights should be left alone.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students understand how a neuron learns through the iterative updating of parameters based on training data. If students struggle here, look at the final table in their activity guide. Guide students to see how the parameter values changed over time, and discuss why those values ended up where they did: help students see that the parameters changed in response to differences between the predicted output and the actual output found in the training data.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a2d2189-518a-4537-82bb-e7bf0f850697",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6cb6e59-e06b-4cf5-8a01-03cb44a549a3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48abdf90-3e19-433c-9897-110f70cdb764",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48abdf90-3e19-433c-9897-110f70cdb764",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6a3ed5e4-0f5f-43e6-8a96-792a5680fba6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21b17fbf-38db-47ea-a4ff-8dfe6683aaff",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _web scraping_."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21b17fbf-38db-47ea-a4ff-8dfe6683aaff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f468f6c-6bbb-4582-9491-a064ae0bb69f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3364985-b679-47e1-a236-a81057f0a0d3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Companies building AI models often get training data by scraping the internet. Think about everything you have ever posted to the internet: photos, messages, comments, videos. If it’s on the internet, it’s possible that someone could scrape it.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3364985-b679-47e1-a236-a81057f0a0d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f468f6c-6bbb-4582-9491-a064ae0bb69f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d1124dc-bc12-465b-ac0a-79fe33ff0c05",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you post something publicly on the internet, should anyone be allowed to use it for training AI?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share their intuitions about how their online posts are used and whether making something public is the same as giving permission. Strong responses will take a position and support it with a reason connected to consent, privacy, or ownership, even if students do not use those words yet. For example, students who think this is reasonable might argue that anything posted publicly is fair to use because anyone in the world can already see it. Students who disagree might point out that people post to share with friends, not with the intent to train AI, or that they posted something when they were much younger and would not post it today. There are no right or wrong answers: the primary goal is to surface students’ initial reactions about data and permission before introducing the vocabulary to describe it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d1124dc-bc12-465b-ac0a-79fe33ff0c05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f468f6c-6bbb-4582-9491-a064ae0bb69f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c5ce1d1-d766-461b-a82a-2a0ad0032ec3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we'll explore where the data that trains AI comes from and how to decide what data is ethical and responsible to use.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c5ce1d1-d766-461b-a82a-2a0ad0032ec3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f468f6c-6bbb-4582-9491-a064ae0bb69f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7a7241f-3c40-49a6-9073-ed88c47cf78a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a privacy engineer.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7a7241f-3c40-49a6-9073-ed88c47cf78a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f468f6c-6bbb-4582-9491-a064ae0bb69f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74d642ef-36d1-44c2-932c-ceac125dfa27",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Privacy engineers are software engineers who specialize in protecting people's personal information. They decide what data an app collects, who can see it, how it gets anonymized, and when it gets deleted, and they work with lawyers to turn privacy laws into working code. As AI systems train on more and more personal data, privacy engineers are at the forefront of making sure that data is collected and used responsibly. Today, you'll get to think like one.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74d642ef-36d1-44c2-932c-ceac125dfa27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f468f6c-6bbb-4582-9491-a064ae0bb69f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6569148-dda0-4e37-8e43-07f73302635f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6569148-dda0-4e37-8e43-07f73302635f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9f468f6c-6bbb-4582-9491-a064ae0bb69f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6dd7ca3b-5e0e-44d7-b259-9d1f49668775",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore where AI training data comes from and what makes it responsible or ethical to use. Students evaluate real-world data sources through the lenses of consent, privacy, ownership, and bias.\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Evaluating Data Sources"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6dd7ca3b-5e0e-44d7-b259-9d1f49668775",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b615dbe4-cfd8-4d78-a468-8d5ce21a8bbd",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are lots of places where data for AI can be found. Today, we’ll  decide what data sources are appropriate through four lenses: consent, privacy, ownership, and bias.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b615dbe4-cfd8-4d78-a468-8d5ce21a8bbd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbd66181-3a2a-42c3-9ea0-0f62a0f08000",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through each of the four lenses, describing the central question for each.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute a copy of the Datasets for AI Activity Guide to each pair of students.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This activity involves students discussing and debating datasets ethics in pairs. You can also consider forming groups of three or four so that students can debate their ideas in larger groups.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbd66181-3a2a-42c3-9ea0-0f62a0f08000",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11e6fa80-c467-4acb-ad1e-e5ec5b9ff716",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "On your handout, you'll see eight datasets. Each one describes a real kind of data that someone might want to use to train an AI. We'll look at the first case together. Then, you and your partner will get two cases to judge on your own.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11e6fa80-c467-4acb-ad1e-e5ec5b9ff716",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9eba7ce-60ac-4b32-844c-316750cb2948",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what students will do in the activity. For each case students analyze, students will first evaluate their biggest concern: consent, privacy, ownership, or bias. They might have multiple concerns, but should pick the one that they feel matters most. Then, students will decide if the data is OK to use, not OK, or OK only if certain conditions are true (for example, “only if faces are blurred”). Then, students will explain their decision.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9eba7ce-60ac-4b32-844c-316750cb2948",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b88543a-d310-40f1-86eb-b28944492ce8",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There’s no perfect answer here. What matters is that you consider the possible issues and can explain your thinking.\n\nIn the first case, a company trains a chatbot on millions of public posts and photos.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b88543a-d310-40f1-86eb-b28944492ce8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0f54d75-3c0a-4163-8385-b8dadc447822",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which is the biggest concern? What’s your verdict?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Facilitate a discussion where students evaluate possible concerns and make decisions. The goal is to model the kind of discussion and debate that students will then try in groups. There isn’t a single right answer, but strong responses might mention consent as a likely concern: posts are public, but people might have shared them with the intent of letting friends see posts, not wanting them to be used for training AI. Students should pick a verdict and defend their position. For example, a strong response might say that this use case is OK with conditions (such as using adult accounts only, letting people choose not to have their posts included in training, or removing usernames or other identifying information)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0f54d75-3c0a-4163-8385-b8dadc447822",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0dafec89-ca67-479d-b375-52641d5c2f97",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One common way to give users more control over how their data is used is to let them opt-in or opt-out of certain uses of their data.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0dafec89-ca67-479d-b375-52641d5c2f97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34690bae-4b5b-4a7f-8191-e8d70dfe3d08",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _opt-in_ and _opt-out_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34690bae-4b5b-4a7f-8191-e8d70dfe3d08",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71fe8947-9602-4f08-86b0-e6a80f79fd59",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Different companies will have different policies: some might only train on your data if you opt-in, while others might require you to opt-out if you don’t want your data used. You can consider opt-in and opt-out as possible conditions when evaluating your datasets.\n\nNow, you’ll have a chance to evaluate some other datasets.\n\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students seem confused by the toggle switch, explain in an opt-in system, the switch starts on gray, and it's up to you to turn it on.\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71fe8947-9602-4f08-86b0-e6a80f79fd59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ef009e7-8dd2-4508-97ca-54815da3f67d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Evaluating Datasets part of the activity guide. For each of two cases, students should discuss and debate, then fill in the table. You can invite groups to either pick two cases that are of interest to them (other than “A”, which was completed together), or you can assign specific cases to different groups so that every case is covered.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did your group decide? What conditions did you come up with?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Take few minutes to debrief at the end of the activity, giving students an opportunity to share their findings. Encourage students who worked on the same case to bring up different ideas, letting students argue for their position and defend their arguments. Remind students that there isn’t a single right answer to these questions, but it’s still important to consider possible issues with using a dataset before incorporating it into an AI model.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Adjust the share-out to the time you have. With more time, aim to hear every case at least once: call on one group per case, B through H, and invite a second group to respond if they disagree. With less time, pick just two or three cases and go deeper. Choose ones where groups reached different verdicts, or cases that usually spark debate, like B (yearbook photos), E (subway cameras), or G (novels). Either way, ask each group for its verdict and biggest concern, and make sure students hear at least one \"OK with conditions\" answer, so they see how conditions can make a data source responsible to use.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ef009e7-8dd2-4508-97ca-54815da3f67d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b221c5c-0be9-4c24-8985-126d1bcbdfb1",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b221c5c-0be9-4c24-8985-126d1bcbdfb1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a3f8781-234b-4b40-87a1-fccc555ca451",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a3f8781-234b-4b40-87a1-fccc555ca451",
        "lesson_activity.key": "83504ab1-7b48-4194-972d-659e28e30ba3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d01dc2fb-2853-4ef6-8ee2-cf1cd05fad2b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d01dc2fb-2853-4ef6-8ee2-cf1cd05fad2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21560767-f077-4eae-aca1-736b8220fd6d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0dc9cda3-ea24-49dc-bac0-531226cd6c11",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *You’re building a rover to explore Mars. What kinds of decisions does your rover need to make?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students are introduced to the scenario for today’s lesson. Students consider the decisions that a computer might have to make, in a situation where the rover’s distance from Earth means that it needs to make decisions on its own rather than relying on humans to make all decisions. There isn’t a single right answer: students might suggest that the rover needs to decide where and how to drive, how fast to go, whether it’s safe to continue, what objects are interesting to explore scientifically, or how to respond when something goes wrong. Help students see that there are a variety of decisions that a rover might need to make based on the inputs available to it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0dc9cda3-ea24-49dc-bac0-531226cd6c11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dfce25dc-ea47-4572-8c9b-4dd530945846"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39b0a494-f288-42e1-b485-f3c508e698b7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll look at how we can use artificial neurons to build systems that make these kinds of decisions intelligently.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39b0a494-f288-42e1-b485-f3c508e698b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dfce25dc-ea47-4572-8c9b-4dd530945846"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7fadc59-eaf1-4ee6-8872-52806f55493d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7fadc59-eaf1-4ee6-8872-52806f55493d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dfce25dc-ea47-4572-8c9b-4dd530945846"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d8dc683-cf80-4726-9173-c0a7be22d615",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn what a neural network is and use a neural network to compute answers to regression problems.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _neural network_.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Neural Networks for Regression"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d8dc683-cf80-4726-9173-c0a7be22d615",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6dcb71c0-db0d-4aa3-af1f-b3e8d2757427",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "So far in this unit, you’ve seen how individual neurons can make calculations based on their inputs. By putting multiple neurons together into a network, we can build systems that can solve even more complex problems.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6dcb71c0-db0d-4aa3-af1f-b3e8d2757427",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7e99367-cd01-4386-a5c1-8299514d8564",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that neural networks are divided into “layers.” Information moves through the layers of a neural network from left to right.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "While neural networks can also refer to biological neural networks, in the context of computer science and AI, the term “neural network” is usually used to mean “artificial neural network”: a network made up of artificial neurons.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7e99367-cd01-4386-a5c1-8299514d8564",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bf03700-88e4-4b47-beac-37bd4d847f9f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _input layer_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show that the input layer is the leftmost layer of the neural network. Each unit of the layer represents a numeric input to the network. These input units don’t perform any calculations: they just store input numbers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _output layer_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show that the output layer is the rightmost layer of the neural network. This layer represents the output of the neural network.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out on the diagram that each unit in the input layer is connected to each unit of the output layer. Each unit in the output layer performs the calculations of an artificial neuron: taking a weighted sum of the inputs and adding a bias.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario for the first part of the activity. Students will explore how neural networks can solve regression problems, where the goal is to predict a number. The regression problem is to have the Mars rover decide its speed based on three inputs: rock height, ground slope, sand firmness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to suggest how they think any of these variables should affect the rover’s speed. Students might say that when rocks are taller, the rover should move more slowly, since it needs to be more careful of bigger rocks. Students might say that when the ground is sloped more, the rover should move more slowly, to avoid losing control. Students might say that when the sand is firmer, the rover can move more quickly because it has more stability.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how the regression problem could be organized in a neural network. The network has an input layer with one unit per input: there are three input variables, so the input layer has three units. The network has an output layer with a single unit representing the speed. The neural network will learn what weights and bias the output neuron should use to calculate speed based on the inputs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The neural network here is structurally the same as what students have already seen with artificial neurons. The last time students worked with artificial neurons, they focused on inputs that were binary (0 or 1). In the upcoming activity, students will see an artificial neuron used for regression where the inputs can take on real-numbered values.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bf03700-88e4-4b47-beac-37bd4d847f9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c754f9a-6dd0-4455-a635-6fc7d906752f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, you’ll have a chance to try calculating what a neural network would calculate to decide on the rover’s speed.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c754f9a-6dd0-4455-a635-6fc7d906752f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fab623f3-6dfc-4c04-ac48-dd6886892de5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute a copy of the Mars Rover Activity Guide to each pair.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Calculating Speed section of the Activity Guide. Students should stop before moving on to the Finding Rock Samples section.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students calculate regression output with their neural network, help them with places where they might get stuck:\n- Students might be confused about the overall structure of the neural network. Help students see that the three input units (x1, x2, and x3) store the input values (rock height, ground slope, sand firmness) and don’t perform any calculations. Then the output unit (speed) calculates its value by the formula shown in the activity guide: multiplying each input value by its weight and adding the bias.\n- Students might struggle with knowing what values to plug into the formula. Each input needs to be multiplied by its corresponding weight: help students find the input values in the bottom table and identify the weight for that input in the middle table.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look at the weights and bias used in this neural network. How do their values help to predict a good speed for the rover?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on the calculations they performed and reason about why the neural network’s structure helps it to make good predictions for speed. Strong responses will interpret the weights of the neural network to explain its behavior: the weight associated with rock height is a negative number, which means that as the rock height input increases, the speed will decrease. Similarly, the ground slope weight is a negative number, which also decreases the network’s output when the ground slope increases. Sand firmness has a weight with a positive value, which means that increasing sand firmness will increase the rover’s speed. The bias acts as a baseline speed that influences the overall speed no matter what the input values are. If students struggle to identify this, point them back to the two example inputs they just calculated and have them explain why the speeds are different in terms of the inputs. Help students see how the weights determine how changing the input values affects the output."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fab623f3-6dfc-4c04-ac48-dd6886892de5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bdc7ea7b-1a28-415e-8783-39beed808a26",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn how a neural network could be structured for classification problems and use a neural network to compute answers to classification problems.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Neural Networks for Classification"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bdc7ea7b-1a28-415e-8783-39beed808a26",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93f21f91-0d00-4295-963b-1cbb02814992",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We can also use neural networks to solve classification problems.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93f21f91-0d00-4295-963b-1cbb02814992",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9f5bac6-cf79-4a17-b1dc-ea707b32c05f",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario for the second part of the activity. Students will explore how neural networks can solve classification problems, where the goal is to predict a category. The classification problem is to have the Mars rover decide what to do when it encounters a rock. You can point out that round rocks are potentially interesting for the rover to explore (they could be signs of water on Mars) and that firmer rocks are harder to take samples from.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9f5bac6-cf79-4a17-b1dc-ea707b32c05f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c62d99f3-2722-4ea8-80ea-47ea80591959",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a classification problem, a machine learning model predicts a category. In this case, the model will pick a category that determines what action the rover should take: skip the rock and move on, take a photo of the rock, or take a sample of the rock.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c62d99f3-2722-4ea8-80ea-47ea80591959",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52b5578e-3863-417b-9a10-56a671d64189",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show how the classification problem could be organized in a neural network. The network has an input layer with two units: each corresponds to one of the two inputs to the problem. This network has an output layer with three units, since the classifier needs to make a prediction that is one of three categories.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out that each of the output neurons is its own artificial neuron performing its own calculation. Each output neuron is connected to the inputs, each output neuron has its own weights, each output neuron will calculate a weighted sum, and each output neuron has its own bias."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52b5578e-3863-417b-9a10-56a671d64189",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1aa0cf8c-d560-454d-9d38-5a9a370dcce3",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This neural network has an output neuron for each of the different categories we might predict. Once we’ve calculated a value for each of the three categories, we can look for the one with the biggest output value and choose that as the prediction that our model makes.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1aa0cf8c-d560-454d-9d38-5a9a370dcce3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0ac2bdcb-bfd5-4a5a-965d-2145d280324b",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Finding Rock Samples section of the Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students calculate classification output with their neural network, help them with places where they might get stuck:\n- Students might struggle with the structure of this neural network, since this is the first time students see a network that has multiple output units. Help students see that each output unit performs its own separate calculation: each output unit has its own weights and bias and calculates its own value.\n- If students don’t know where to begin, encourage them to start by picking just one output unit (for example, the “Skip” unit) and calculating its value based on the inputs and the weights/bias for that output unit. This calculation is very similar to what students did in the previous stage of this activity. The difference is that, once students are done, they need to repeat the calculation for the other two output units, using the correct set of weights/biases for each.\n- Some students might not know how to make a final prediction after calculating values for each of the three output units. In this case, students can predict based on whichever value is highest: ask students to look at the output values for the Skip, Photo, and Sample neurons and determine which of the three has the largest value. The prediction will be the name of the category that has the largest value.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look at the weights and biases used in this neural network. How do their values help to predict a good action for the rover to take?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on the calculations they performed and reason about why the neural network’s structure helps it to make good predictions for what action to take. Strong responses will identify how the weights and biases vary across the three different neurons and connect that to the goal of the problem. For example, students might point out that the Skip neuron uses a negative weight for the Roundness input: this means that if the rock is rounder, the Skip value gets smaller, so the network is less likely to want to skip round rocks. Similarly, students might point out that the Photo and Sample neurons have positive weights for the Roundness input: this means if the rock is rounder, the network is more likely to predict that the rover should photograph or sample the rock. Students might also note that the Sample neuron has a negative weight for the Firmness input: firmer rocks are harder to sample, so it makes sense that if the firmness of the rock increases, the network is less likely to want to sample it. If students struggle to identify these patterns, ask them a specific question about the input (for example, “If the rock is rounder, which output values will increase?”) and help them think through the formula used by the neural network to determine the answer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0ac2bdcb-bfd5-4a5a-965d-2145d280324b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03b70a99-fe3e-423f-9a4c-920e98c6ba89",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In today’s activity, we looked at neural networks that had already learned weights and biases that do a good job of solving problems. But in the future, we’ll need to use data to train the neural networks ourselves to help them learn the right weights and biases to use!\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03b70a99-fe3e-423f-9a4c-920e98c6ba89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "974cac50-105e-418a-9032-6447a6347452",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "974cac50-105e-418a-9032-6447a6347452",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90845027-c909-46a8-ad70-8d2c015f7f94",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 1.\n- Level 1: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks students to interpret how changing a neural network input affects its output.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 1 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students understand the connection between inputs, weights, and outputs of a neural network. Students should be able to identify a relationship between an input and an output in a neural network. If students struggle here, look at the formula for an artificial neuron together and discuss what happens to the output as the inputs get larger and smaller. Help students see the role that weights play in determining this relationship.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90845027-c909-46a8-ad70-8d2c015f7f94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5ca979e4-1680-4f17-b43f-0057ad02dc64"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4dab8674-2cf5-4d5f-8d47-67d50eb8691a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4dab8674-2cf5-4d5f-8d47-67d50eb8691a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa3e3fab-ad0c-41a3-a260-55cb63d3abc3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a952b020-61ae-4c2e-aad0-7090333d9821",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In Lesson 5 you computed the Mars rover’s speed from a network whose weights the engineers had set by hand. Tall rocks and steep ground are what slow the rover down, so the network reads two sensors: the camera’s estimate of the rock height ahead, in inches, and the tilt sensor’s ground slope, in degrees. The Lesson 5 network had a third input, sand firmness; today we leave it out, because the sand on our imaginary practice field is the same everywhere, so that reading never changes. The engineers’ numbers are on the slide: minus 4 for rock height, minus 2 for ground slope, and a bias of 90.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a952b020-61ae-4c2e-aad0-7090333d9821",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988bde46-8ed8-4b43-b3b6-f577c39fc490"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8284dc4b-5ccc-4887-9785-779b85f24ce5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *The engineers set these numbers by hand. How would you find out whether they are right?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students take a position on how a guessed model can be checked as they share their answers with the class. Strong responses will want evidence from the rover itself: drive it, compare the speed it managed with the speed the network predicted, keep a record of each rover drive, and change the numbers when the predictions are wrong. Encourage students to say what “wrong” or “incorrect” would look like and which way they would change a number. Help students see that checking a model means comparing its predictions with what really happened. That comparison between what was predicted and what happened tells you how to adjust the model."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8284dc4b-5ccc-4887-9785-779b85f24ce5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988bde46-8ed8-4b43-b3b6-f577c39fc490"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab4062ad-9e95-4b44-a931-e2c91ac757c9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "That network is a regression model: numbers in, one number out. Today the rover’s own drives will teach it its numbers. You will see how a neural network learns to solve a regression problem.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab4062ad-9e95-4b44-a931-e2c91ac757c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988bde46-8ed8-4b43-b3b6-f577c39fc490"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "076e14f8-b1d3-4a47-ac6b-e709c4905d2c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a robotics engineer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "076e14f8-b1d3-4a47-ac6b-e709c4905d2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988bde46-8ed8-4b43-b3b6-f577c39fc490"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6b863ee-4741-4596-acb7-3dd23233e5ee",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you will be playing the role of robotics engineers on the Mars rover team. A robotics engineer designs and builds robots and the software that runs them. They turn a robot’s sensor readings into decisions, test those decisions, and improve them with data from every run (or drive). Robotics engineers work on warehouse robots, factory arms, surgical robots, self-driving cars, planetary rovers, and beyond!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6b863ee-4741-4596-acb7-3dd23233e5ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988bde46-8ed8-4b43-b3b6-f577c39fc490"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8221ab49-0342-4496-9432-013eb7033867",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8221ab49-0342-4496-9432-013eb7033867",
        "lesson_activity.key": "988bde46-8ed8-4b43-b3b6-f577c39fc490"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1bf3a73-17a5-4859-be48-c0cf32aa1311",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students compute the hand-set network’s predictions for three logged rover drives, find the errors, and decide which way each weight should move.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Checking the Engineers' Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1bf3a73-17a5-4859-be48-c0cf32aa1311",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88369cd9-b702-4d4b-9d3c-5dab0ff4d882",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s imagine the rover has crossed the practice field three times, and the team logged each drive’s terrain readings and the speed that got it through. Check the engineers’ model: work out what it predicts for each drive, compare that with what the rover did, and decide which way each number should move.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88369cd9-b702-4d4b-9d3c-5dab0ff4d882",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3739cf95-19f7-4e9a-84d9-9528fb52eb63",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Practice Drives Activity Guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students made this kind of adjustment by hand on the Training Neurons Activity Guide in Lesson 3: a small change to each parameter in the direction that lowers the error. If they kept their completed copies, have them out for reference."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3739cf95-19f7-4e9a-84d9-9528fb52eb63",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f56426e-22c0-4cd1-8167-c959773b9756",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Practice Drives Activity Guide: the three equation strips and the three questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Some pairs may experience a challenge when multiplying by a negative weight. Point to the worked example on the guide: (−4 × 1) is −4, and adding a negative number takes speed away.\n- Some pairs may write the error with the wrong sign. Remind them that the error is the actual speed minus the predicted speed, so a rover that went slower than predicted has a negative error.\n- Some pairs may be unsure what “decrease” means for a weight that is already negative. Say that “decrease” means a smaller number, so a negative weight that decreases becomes a bigger negative number.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What if the rover had logged a thousand drives? How would you want the weights adjusted? Who should do such arithmetic?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students generalize what they did on three drives to many as they share their answers with the class. Strong responses will keep the same move, a small adjustment after each drive in the direction its error points, and repeat it drive after drive, letting the weights settle where the errors stop pointing anywhere. Encourage students to notice that one big jump from one drive could create inaccuracies, which is why the steps stay small. Help students see that the arithmetic is simple but there is a lot of it to manage and compute: a great job for the computer."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f56426e-22c0-4cd1-8167-c959773b9756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59dabe05-42b2-4d4c-9c03-1fff4a854111",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This way of adjusting a model, a small step after each example in the direction that lowers the error, is called “gradient descent.”",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59dabe05-42b2-4d4c-9c03-1fff4a854111",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04929c18-e152-4e05-8f89-7b1eb280f9c4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *gradient descent*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04929c18-e152-4e05-8f89-7b1eb280f9c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1557a94-4a3f-4d20-912e-abdeae62e021",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Gradient descent is a training algorithm that adjusts a model’s parameters a small step at a time, always in the direction that lowers the error. Gradient descent is what you just did by hand: look at one drive, find the error, nudge each weight a little in the direction that would have made the error smaller, and move on to the next rover drive.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1557a94-4a3f-4d20-912e-abdeae62e021",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef78ba03-9380-49cc-b92d-d930b760bd44",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The code’s model is called `SGDRegressor`. The S stands for stochastic: the algorithm takes its step after each single drive, in a shuffled order, rather than after looking at all of the drives at once.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef78ba03-9380-49cc-b92d-d930b760bd44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ceabebf5-9344-430e-b1c7-a1bc1e933ac8",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students train the rover’s speed network on the logged drives with gradient descent, compare its predictions with the engineers’ model’s, and watch the predictions converge.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Training the Speed Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ceabebf5-9344-430e-b1c7-a1bc1e933ac8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "761d03e5-7a42-4957-9c51-096ef6c9cd5f",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "With the nine-line program on the slide, a rover learns its own numbers from its logged drives. It is the program you will complete in Level 1.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "761d03e5-7a42-4957-9c51-096ef6c9cd5f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "210d3e7b-68f2-4cc9-93c9-69eb60bf7eb2",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the nine rows: the three imports bring in the neuron, `SGDRegressor`, the scaler, and the pipeline that joins them. inputs holds the twelve logged rover drives as (a) rock height and (b) ground slope, and outputs the speed that got the rover through each one. model is the untrained network, a scaler followed by one neuron; TODO 1 trains it on the drives, which runs gradient descent over every drive many times over; TODO 2 asks the trained network for the speed on a new drive, rock height 2 and ground slope 10."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "210d3e7b-68f2-4cc9-93c9-69eb60bf7eb2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5dcfa03-0d1f-41bb-a834-8104fac42732",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As in earlier lessons, we are building a line on a graph, so to speak, in code, and using it to predict an outcome.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Without the scaler, the program’s predictions are unreliable: they come out wrong and differ on every run. That is why the pipeline code is given rather than written."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5dcfa03-0d1f-41bb-a834-8104fac42732",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1b0137f-a722-4380-9236-14b8b76c1b59",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This pipeline slide builds the model in two steps joined by `make_pipeline`. First, the scaler rescales rock height and ground slope so that inches and degrees count alike. Second, the neuron, `SGDRegressor`, learns from the scaled numbers. Next, calling `model.fit` trains both steps. Finally, `model.predict` sends a new drive through both the scaler and the neuron.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may expect a one-line model, such as `SGDRegressor()`. Discuss this slide a minute or two. You may remind them that the scaling step is the feature scaling they met in Unit 4, Lesson 8, now done inside the model."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1b0137f-a722-4380-9236-14b8b76c1b59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e88820d-fadb-4b55-9cef-b6de07955015",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *convergence*.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e88820d-fadb-4b55-9cef-b6de07955015",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e84b18c-c18d-4f52-89e1-4248eb039732",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Gradient descent takes small steps, so how does the computer know when to stop? It watches the error: when another pass over the drives no longer lowers the error by much, training has “converged.” In Level 2 you will watch this happen: a program trains the network with one, ten, a hundred and then a thousand passes.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e84b18c-c18d-4f52-89e1-4248eb039732",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56c749c1-e827-4d54-8749-6672bed8acaa",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Training the Speed Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56c749c1-e827-4d54-8749-6672bed8acaa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "98cfeb1e-04dd-46c7-9af2-0cdcc5fc33bf",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "98cfeb1e-04dd-46c7-9af2-0cdcc5fc33bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "12309885-0d73-4049-82db-7d9a5e9b60ad",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 1–3.\n- **Level 1:** Students train the speed network on the logged drives and print its predictions for two new drives.\n- **Level 2:** Students predict, then watch, how the prediction changes as the number of passes grows.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding on how the neuron predicts and what training did.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who are unsure of the syntax for training the network or making a prediction can ask AI Tutor, or you can point them to the Coding Tip, which shows the pattern with a placeholder.\n- Some students may call fit without both lists, or predict with a plain list. Remind them that fit takes the inputs and the outputs, and that predict takes a list of drives, so one drive is a list inside a list.\n- Some students may read Level 2’s warning about reaching the maximum number of iterations as an error. Say that it is the program reporting that training stopped before it converged, which is exactly what they are watching.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 3 asks students to describe how the neuron turns the two inputs into a speed, what gradient descent did to the parameters during training, and how the Level 2 output showed convergence. Strong responses give both halves of the training story: the weights moved a small step after each drive in the direction that lowered the error, and the prediction stopped changing between a hundred passes and a thousand. A response that gives only one of those two halves is at Level 1 of the rubric; one that describes only the weighted sum has not yet answered the training question. If a student says the weights “got better”, ask what the error did at each step."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "12309885-0d73-4049-82db-7d9a5e9b60ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "666628f3-3cb6-4b19-a315-53047188efb8",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the output of the finished program."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "666628f3-3cb6-4b19-a315-53047188efb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3279cbb-17e1-418e-bce7-4aab85351183",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The rover’s actual drives taught it different numbers from the engineers’ model: 68 instead of 62 on the first new drive, and 36 instead of 14 on the second. Its log for those two new drives says 68 and 36. The trained model was right on both. The engineers’ model was off by 6 and 22.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3279cbb-17e1-418e-bce7-4aab85351183",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d017371e-35e2-44a7-9093-6366b116e85e",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look at the four `max_iter` lines: 5 after one pass, 26 after ten, 64 after a hundred and 68 after a thousand. That is convergence: more passes stopped changing the answer. The first two lines wander by a point or two from run to run, because the drives are shuffled before each pass.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d017371e-35e2-44a7-9093-6366b116e85e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "57d3b182-22d0-4aa6-a212-c4c1af564db3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "04df9210-7ff2-4120-8fe8-511181584e8f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "04df9210-7ff2-4120-8fe8-511181584e8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0dffe35f-50db-4e99-8b50-cc9c79918e6b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "08f95a54-f28a-4cc5-875e-9d14f8a8a782",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the scenario for the day’s lesson: a neural network that has to sort recycled items into different categories.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you were building a robot to sort these items into aluminum, glass, and plastic bins, what rules might it use?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students propose possible rules that a robot could use to sort items into different recycling bins. Strong responses will recognize that a robot needs measurable features (like weight, transparency, color, or whether a magnet sticks) to make a sorting decision. Help students brainstorm some possible features if they get stuck. Students might also consider specific examples of objects, like a jar or milk jug, to come up with simple rules. Students might propose general rules like “heavy items are glass” that work in some cases but breaks down on some other items, so strong responses will note that a good classification procedure needs to combine more than one measurement."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "08f95a54-f28a-4cc5-875e-9d14f8a8a782",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb28eb7d-ac43-45cb-8768-00823fd7e7d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1de232e-bd2c-40f0-ac0f-39451c4087b6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The rules to automatically classify items can be complicated, so today we’ll build a neural network to help us with the process. The network will look each item’s weight and transparency to predict where the item belongs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1de232e-bd2c-40f0-ac0f-39451c4087b6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb28eb7d-ac43-45cb-8768-00823fd7e7d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "740b5843-4442-4385-822b-90ee2f9eb547",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "740b5843-4442-4385-822b-90ee2f9eb547",
        "lesson_activity.key": "cb28eb7d-ac43-45cb-8768-00823fd7e7d6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a340584e-796f-467b-90de-b44245ff5f3a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students see how a neural network with one output neuron per class makes a classification decision. They calculate each neuron’s score for an item, identify the winning category as the highest score, find the item that is classified incorrectly, and decide which way each weight should move to fix it.",
        "name": "Predicting with a Neural Network Classifier"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a340584e-796f-467b-90de-b44245ff5f3a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3aa9cffb-ddfd-46f4-831a-c22b91d8409b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The robot’s network was trained on only a few items, so it still makes mistakes. Your job is to check how well it sorts. The robot measures two features of each item: its weight and its transparency. Those are the inputs. This network has three output neurons, one for each bin: Aluminum, Glass, and Plastic. You’ll calculate a score for each output neuron, and the highest score will be the output the robot chooses.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3aa9cffb-ddfd-46f4-831a-c22b91d8409b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "580c473c-9f5d-4b6c-b971-d16d5ca20b2f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Robot Recycling Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Robot Recycling Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>**Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Some weights and biases are negative, so some students might forget a negative sign in their calculations. Walk through one example: for the Small Jar's Glass neuron, for example, (7 × 4) is 28, (−1 × 8) is −8, and adding the bias of −17 gives 3.\n- Some may forget to add the bias, especially when the bias is negative. Point out that every score ends with the bias, so the Glass bias of −17 pulls its score down for every item.\n- Some pairs may think they made an error when the Small Jar comes out wrong. Point out that sometimes, neural networks can make mistakes. They’ll be asked about the neural network’s mistakes in the questions at the end of the activity guide.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Help students think through the math for the last question in the guide.\n- Point students to the Glass and Plastic neurons for the Small Jar. Aluminum scored −34, so it is far behind and changing it won't help. Glass scored 3 and Plastic scored 15, so the gap to close is 12 points.\n- Ask which parameters change a score the most. A weight is multiplied by its input, so a change of 1 to a transparency weight moves the Small Jar's score by 8, a change of 1 to an ounces weight moves it by 4, and a change of 1 to a bias moves it by 1.\n- Good answers raise the Glass neuron's transparency weight or lower the Plastic neuron's, or raise the Glass bias. Accept any reasoned answer that makes the Glass value go up or the Plastic value go down, and listen for students who say why. The strongest responses say the Small Jar is very clear, so the transparency weights matter most.\n- Students who change an ounces weight may notice it also moves the Green Bottle, which has 10 ounces. That is a fine observation to bring up: fixing one mistake can produce mistakes for other items, but students should see that small adjustments are less likely to change other results than large adjustments."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "580c473c-9f5d-4b6c-b971-d16d5ca20b2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ec8b3d5-a860-4345-a744-ca1af165bdf5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let’s do the first scan together. The red can weighs 0.5 ounces and has a transparency of 0. Each neuron multiplies those inputs by its own weights, then adds its bias. Follow the two arrows into Aluminum: −1 times 0.5, plus −5 times 0, plus 10, gives 9.5. Glass and Plastic do the same thing with their own weights. Aluminum has the highest score, so the robot sorts the can into Aluminum, and that’s correct. Now try the Green Bottle and Small Jar on your own.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ec8b3d5-a860-4345-a744-ca1af165bdf5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8779223c-09da-4fe6-8578-a4c7b1fc143c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Fixing one weight worked for three items. What if the robot made hundreds of mistakes on thousands of items? How could we fix the weights without doing it all by hand?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that adjusting weights by hand doesn’t scale: each fix can affect other items, and checking every item after every change would take far too long. Students should connect this to training from earlier lessons, where the computer repeatedly checks its predictions and nudges every weight a little at a time to produce increasingly accurate results. This is gradient descent, applied now to a network that performs classification."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8779223c-09da-4fe6-8578-a4c7b1fc143c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9f64543-8d29-41b0-8398-5236fa8953d6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This is the same gradient descent you used to train the rover’s speed network: make a prediction, check it against the right answer, and nudge each weight a little to reduce the error. On the next slide you’ll see a tool called `SGDClassifier`. SGD stands for Stochastic Gradient Descent. It’s the same training process, now used to choose a bin instead of predict a number.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9f64543-8d29-41b0-8398-5236fa8953d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d31cebf-fb8b-431b-b80f-2748abc081f1",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students train the recycling robot's neural network and analyze its predictions as it trains for longer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example code to show students how to construct a machine learning pipeline with a neural network classifier. Show students that `make_pipeline` constructs a pipeline that includes both a scaler (to ensure features of different scales are handled appropriately) and an `SGDClassifier`. Point out that SGD stands for Stochastic Gradient Descent, the same training process from earlier lessons: the classifier checks its predictions and nudges each weight a little at a time. “Stochastic” means it works through the items in random order. The network it trains has the same shape as the sorting network on the worksheet: two inputs connected to one output neuron per bin. Once the pipeline is built, it can be used to fit data and make predictions in the same way students have made predictions with other models.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Training the Sorting Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d31cebf-fb8b-431b-b80f-2748abc081f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9d92868c-3eba-4721-80c1-bbbd4e0d0669",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Training the Sorting Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9d92868c-3eba-4721-80c1-bbbd4e0d0669",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de2d70aa-5f2a-40d6-9c0c-d6654e950e05",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de2d70aa-5f2a-40d6-9c0c-d6654e950e05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7589563b-ee22-4397-bae9-16e29e9635cb",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Levels 1–3.\n- **Level 1:** Students train the sorting network and print its predictions for the three new items.\n- **Level 2:** Students run a program that trains the network five times with increasing iterations (1 to 10,000), identify when the small jar’s prediction stops changing, and compare the results to their Level 1 network.\n- **Level 3:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding explaining how the trained network decides which bin an item belongs in and why increasing the number of iterations improved its predictions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who are unsure of the syntax for training the network or making a prediction can ask AI Tutor. You can also point these students to the Coding Tip, which shows the pattern with a placeholder.\n- Some students may call `fit` without both lists, or predict with a plain list. Remind them that `fit` takes the inputs and the outputs, and that predict takes a list of items, so one item is a list inside a list.\n- If students notice that the network in Level 1 incorrect classifies the small jar, point out that the network is trained on a limited amount of data, and so might not product the most accurate results.\n- In Level 2, students try a different number of iterations. Some students may try a small value, such as 5 or 60, and get all three items correct, then find that 50 is wrong. Tell them that with only a few iterations, the Small Jar's answer can flip back and forth, because it sits close to the boundary between Glass and Plastic. Ask them to try a value of at least 200, where the answer stays correct.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 3 asks students to describe how the network uses an item's weight and transparency to choose a bin, what training did to the weights, and how the Level 2 output showed that training longer improved the predictions. Strong responses give both halves of the story: the scaler puts the two readings on the same scale, each bin's neuron computes a weighted sum, and the bin with the highest score wins. Training nudged the weights a small step after each scan in the direction that minimized the loss. The Level 2 output showed the small jar changing from Plastic to Glass between 10 and 100 iterations."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7589563b-ee22-4397-bae9-16e29e9635cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6238d4f5-1714-49f5-9fe5-9627afb8625c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36692a62-19d0-4214-bc3d-893ad58fbb22",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36692a62-19d0-4214-bc3d-893ad58fbb22",
        "lesson_activity.key": "36b1db4c-9c39-48f8-95f1-89ff3ac98912"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63dbf90d-2bcd-4512-8209-873664e58893",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *An urgent-care clinic tested an app that tells patients to “come in now” or to “wait until tomorrow.” It agreed with qualified nurses for 970 of 1,000 patients. Is it ready to launch?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students take a position on whether an app that is right 97 times in 100 is ready for patients as they share their answers with the class. Strong responses will look past the 970 to the other 30 answers on the card and will weigh the two kinds of mistake differently: a patient told to come in loses an afternoon, and a patient told to wait may go without care they need. Point out that 20 of the other 30 were told to wait when they needed care that day, so at 500 patients a week the app would send about 10 people home every week who should have come in. Help students see that a high score is not the same as safe: at the clinic’s scale, a small error rate is many patients."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63dbf90d-2bcd-4512-8209-873664e58893",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91af7f7c-f8db-4e75-8918-8334fa3d7284"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50fe13f3-7468-4e6b-a348-0b48fd5d27e3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A score alone does not tell you whether a system is safe to use. Today, we look at what a team builds around an AI system so that its mistakes are caught before they hurt someone.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The clinic and its app are fictional, written for this lesson; the numbers are chosen to keep the arithmetic clean."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50fe13f3-7468-4e6b-a348-0b48fd5d27e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91af7f7c-f8db-4e75-8918-8334fa3d7284"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92d7275d-1672-49f5-a3da-f7da328027cc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this lesson’s career connection: a trust and safety specialist."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92d7275d-1672-49f5-a3da-f7da328027cc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91af7f7c-f8db-4e75-8918-8334fa3d7284"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e90c6a4-e19e-4704-a4e4-b3a5bafb28bf",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you are on the trust and safety team at that clinic, meeting the week before the app launches. A trust and safety specialist protects the people who use a product: they decide what an AI system may do on its own, what a person must review, and how to catch its mistakes before they spread or cause harm. These teams work wherever software makes decisions about people.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e90c6a4-e19e-4704-a4e4-b3a5bafb28bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91af7f7c-f8db-4e75-8918-8334fa3d7284"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7defa083-af06-4b1c-9fd3-f34a477b4cb9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7defa083-af06-4b1c-9fd3-f34a477b4cb9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "91af7f7c-f8db-4e75-8918-8334fa3d7284"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7884921-2806-4bdf-a8f9-1e14eba637b0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students name realistic ways the clinic’s app can be wrong and the people each mistake would harm.",
        "duration": "12",
        "name": "Find the Failures"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7884921-2806-4bdf-a8f9-1e14eba637b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd85ac24-bfd7-4352-9477-aa5b0f26850a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The clinic is planning on launching the app next week. You are its trust and safety team. The guide tells you what the app does and how many patients the clinic sees. Your first job is to find the failures: the ways the app could be wrong, who each one would harm, and how badly. Stop at the stop sign.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd85ac24-bfd7-4352-9477-aa5b0f26850a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da49aaf6-f240-4974-a214-50b747fdfe2b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a copy of the Launch Review Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete page 1 of the Launch Review Activity Guide, including the failures table and the question under it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- If students aren’t sure what scenarios to consider, encourage them to think about different possible kinds of patients: patients with rare symptoms, young children, or patients with symptoms that change overnight.\n- Some pairs may write a failure without naming a person. Ask: “Who gets that wrong answer?” Every row needs a person or a group in the middle column.\n- Some pairs may write three failures of the same kind. Point them to the cues under the instructions: rare symptoms, young children, symptoms that change overnight.\n- Some pairs may rate every failure dangerous. Ask which of their failures a patient could survive by coming in a day late; that one is serious, not dangerous.\n- If pairs finish early, ask them which of their failures would be hardest for the clinic to notice from the inside.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What could the app get wrong, and who would it harm?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students examine the failures they found as they share their rows with the class. Strong responses will name a specific mistake and a specific group: a wrong wait for a child whose parent described the symptoms vaguely, a wrong wait for a patient with a rare illness the app has seen few examples of. Collect the groups on the board. Help students see that the dangerous failures are the wrong waits, because the patient stays home and no one at the clinic knows, and that they fall on the people the app has the least practice with."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da49aaf6-f240-4974-a214-50b747fdfe2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93a96d83-2ce1-4bd1-a291-fe63660f2739",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Finding the failures is half of the team’s job. The other half is deciding what to build around the app so that those mistakes are caught. There are three kinds of checks to choose from.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93a96d83-2ce1-4bd1-a291-fe63660f2739",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7675f983-b5cf-4e0c-96da-562bb8c8e39f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn three kinds of safeguard and design one of each for the clinic’s app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *safeguard*.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Place the Safeguards"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7675f983-b5cf-4e0c-96da-562bb8c8e39f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "60bfce10-d8e6-4255-8155-57b9786a3dac",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A safeguard is a check built around an AI system to catch its mistakes before they harm someone. At present, the app will keep making its 30 mistakes in every thousand. A safeguard decides what should happen next. We will look at three kinds of safeguards: a person who reviews, a check on the record, and a limit on what the app may do.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "60bfce10-d8e6-4255-8155-57b9786a3dac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93ecbd73-5bb4-4e26-bd25-681cdd2d30f2",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *human review*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93ecbd73-5bb4-4e26-bd25-681cdd2d30f2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b8dabf7-d02e-4f7d-9e0e-6bab31683b82",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Human review is a step where a person looks at an AI system’s decision before it takes effect. At the clinic, that is a nurse reading the app’s answer before the patient sees it. At present, reviewing every answer would be 500 a week. Reviewing every wait is about 200. The waits are the answers that are dangerous when wrong.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may ask why the nurse does not simply answer every patient. The point of the app is to handle the easy cases. Human review keeps a person on the cases where a mistake costs the most."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b8dabf7-d02e-4f7d-9e0e-6bab31683b82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b856aacc-e38d-4ad4-b76b-99488f8af312",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *audit*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b856aacc-e38d-4ad4-b76b-99488f8af312",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a65bb44-d8ed-4c69-a8bd-319986317a16",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "An audit is a regular check of an AI system’s past decisions to find mistakes and patterns of harm. Once a month, the clinic can compare what the app said with what happened to each patient. Counting the wrong waits by symptom and by age group shows where the app fails and who it fails.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a65bb44-d8ed-4c69-a8bd-319986317a16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e180b2dc-66d2-4f5b-95a7-0fca547f5348",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The third kind of safeguard is a limit on what the app can reach or change, often called access control. An app cannot make a mistake with something it is not allowed to touch. On page 2, your team decides which actions the app may take by itself: advising a patient, storing the answers, and booking or canceling a visit.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e180b2dc-66d2-4f5b-95a7-0fca547f5348",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "063ef47f-75c8-4497-b88e-74fb4f20e7d1",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to turn to page 2 of the Launch Review Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete page 2 of the Launch Review Activity Guide: the checkmarks and the three safeguard boxes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Some pairs may put every action under nurse reviews. Ask how many answers one nurse can read in a week. Reviewing every wait is about 200 answers a week, and reviewing every answer is 500.\n- Some pairs may be stuck on the audit. Encourage students to think about what the clinic could compare the app’s advice against once the patient’s visit is over.\n- If pairs finish early, ask them what they would change after the first month’s audit showed the app failing young children most.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which of the app’s answers does a nurse need to see? Why those?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students compare their safeguard designs as they share their answers with the class. Strong responses will send every wait to the nurse, because a wrong wait keeps a patient away, and will accept that the “come in now” answers can stand, because a wrong one costs a visit. Ask whether anyone disagrees with the first row of the table on page 2 of the guide, where the app says “come in now” on its own, and why. Encourage students to say how their audit would find the mistakes the nurse missed. An audit fixes nothing by itself, so ask what the clinic would change once it shows a pattern. Help students see that the three safeguards work together: the review catches a mistake before it reaches a patient, the audit finds the pattern afterwards, and the limit on what the app may do keeps a mistake from spreading."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "063ef47f-75c8-4497-b88e-74fb4f20e7d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cd9bea33-5e13-4cb7-8391-35b315a10d0f",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "An AI system is made safer to use by the checks around it: people who review its riskiest decisions, a record that gets audited, and limits on what it may do on its own. That is how the clinic can launch an app that is wrong 30 times in a thousand.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students may object to AI being used in health decisions at all. Leave room for that position, and let the class hear the range of opinions and values in the room. You can ask: “Do patients have the right to opt out of AI decisions?”"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cd9bea33-5e13-4cb7-8391-35b315a10d0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0fba9b67-9401-4c6b-87b0-e5143ad97c3f",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0fba9b67-9401-4c6b-87b0-e5143ad97c3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "161044cb-f0f6-44cc-9890-f8954e9633a8",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 1.\n- **Level 1:** Students name a failure, who it hurts and a safeguard for a school’s door-camera attendance app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write, look for…\n- Some students may name a failure without a person. Ask who at the school gets the wrong mark, and what happens to them that day.\n- Some students may name a safeguard that does not match their failure. Ask how their safeguard would catch that exact mistake, and who would act on it.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 1 is the Building Trustworthy AI (Stage 2) assessment point. Strong responses name a realistic mistake the attendance app could make, the students it would hurt, and a safeguard that catches that specific mistake, saying who acts and when. A response that names a vague failure or a vague protection is at Level 1 of the rubric, and one that names the mistake and the group but only a general safeguard is at Level 2. If a response names only a general safeguard, ask who would act, on which answers, and when."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "161044cb-f0f6-44cc-9890-f8954e9633a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "06ef09c2-aa08-425c-a7de-3fc255462d1f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9df62a13-e188-4794-8a6b-62665cb94ccf",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9df62a13-e188-4794-8a6b-62665cb94ccf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "efca9014-8cea-4a6d-9004-14310f16c505"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "daace050-42d6-4878-9147-ea78b205cd07",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Present a seating chart for a movie theater. Ask students to consider what makes one seat more preferable than another. Invite a few students to share a specific seat that they would like: some might prefer to be closer to the screen, some might prefer to be further away, though most will likely prefer to be in a central seat number.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which options best complete the rule? Seats are better when row number is [larger/smaller] and when seat number is [larger/smaller].*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students try to come up with a rule that describes which movie seats are better or worse. Some students might express a preference for larger or smaller numbers, but the goal of this discussion is for students to see that there are some preferences that aren’t capable of being expressed by the structure of this rule. For example, students who prefer to be seated in the middle of the theater have no good way to complete the rule: they don’t want the seat number to be larger, they don’t want the seat number to be smaller, they want it to be somewhere in the middle. Strong responses will identify this issue: help students see that the constraints put in place by the structure of the rule don’t allow for describing a student’s preferences."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "daace050-42d6-4878-9147-ea78b205cd07",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e829acf7-3ce6-43b2-be13-c4e78776408b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0738495-ff13-407f-b01b-650e3005fd95",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In AI, we often face a similar problem. When AI is learning rules, we need to make sure that the structure of those rules allows them to learn what we want it to learn. Otherwise, we might end up in a situation where AI has no way to learn what we’re trying to teach it.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0738495-ff13-407f-b01b-650e3005fd95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e829acf7-3ce6-43b2-be13-c4e78776408b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e045f614-1a67-404d-8e06-048b1ba9a298",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e045f614-1a67-404d-8e06-048b1ba9a298",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e829acf7-3ce6-43b2-be13-c4e78776408b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f3082716-db6a-44dc-856d-5d8d82c45981",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students identify the types of patterns that individual neurons cannot solve.\n",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Patterns Learned by Neurons"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f3082716-db6a-44dc-856d-5d8d82c45981",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d455dd0-d66e-4366-ace4-04939c031edc",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Just now, we saw an example where the structure of a rule wasn’t flexible enough to learn a pattern. Let’s now see what that might look like in a neural network.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d455dd0-d66e-4366-ace4-04939c031edc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8186fc9f-d196-4c93-bcc0-249d0c9d7c03",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Present students with an artificial neuron with a single input. For these examples, students can assume a zero bias. Remind students of the math for the neuron: the input value is multiplied by its weight to get the result.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8186fc9f-d196-4c93-bcc0-249d0c9d7c03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3c25baf-1e9a-407b-9b25-c854b7088316",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This neuron has a positive weight. When the weight is positive, increasing the input will increase the output. In other words, a bigger input is “better” for the neuron: bigger inputs increase its output value.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3c25baf-1e9a-407b-9b25-c854b7088316",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9cfdd21f-d38d-45b4-bd8f-27870f968f8f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through an example. If a neuron has a positive weight, then increasing the corresponding input from 1 to 2 will also increase the neuron’s output.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9cfdd21f-d38d-45b4-bd8f-27870f968f8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6022e43e-8afd-49ec-9e60-1f6d080fbdda",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Neurons can also have negative weight. When the weight is negative, increasing the input will decrease the output. In other words, a smaller input is “better” for the neuron: smaller inputs increase its output value.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6022e43e-8afd-49ec-9e60-1f6d080fbdda",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "729d6232-27b0-4d9f-a7a1-8baaad6802dc",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through an example. If a neuron has a negative weight, then the smaller input of 1 will result in a larger output than the larger input of 2.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "729d6232-27b0-4d9f-a7a1-8baaad6802dc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fb29fcf8-0d45-421b-8749-8330ac8b3251",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As a result, a single neuron only has two ways to respond to an input. Either bigger inputs are better, or smaller inputs are better.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fb29fcf8-0d45-421b-8749-8330ac8b3251",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ec1dcd1-1a85-4fd1-87e9-224ebd5bd891",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show an example of a situation where neither “bigger is better” nor “smaller is better” will capture a pattern. In the row of seats in the theater, if a person prefers the seats in the middle, then neither “bigger is better” nor “smaller is better” will represent their preferences.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ec1dcd1-1a85-4fd1-87e9-224ebd5bd891",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c3f44ce-a857-4a80-9b1a-3053304f01b4",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are some patterns that neurons by themselves just can’t learn. In a moment, you’ll have a chance to explore some of these yourself.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c3f44ce-a857-4a80-9b1a-3053304f01b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b818042f-5a60-4223-833e-067aed9d4f21",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute a copy of the Limits of Neurons Activity Guide to each pair.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Limits of Neurons Activity Guide in pairs. Students work through four example problems and construct a neuron to solve the problem or claim that there is no solution.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students construct their neurons, help them with places where they might get stuck:\n- Students might need to be reminded of the formula for computing a neuron’s output. Remind students that each neuron computes the weighted sum of its inputs (the sum of each input multiplied by its corresponding weight) plus the bias.\n- In the Cell Service example, if students struggle to identify the weight and bias, ask students to think about how much the cost increases with each person. Help students see that this value is the weight, as it’s directly related to the number of people. Then, have students think about what bias needs to be added to the result of the multiplication to get the correct cost.\n- In the Song Popularity example, compare the data to the theater seat example students looked at earlier in the lesson. If students struggle, point out to them the similarities in the pattern: more weeks isn’t always better, and more weeks isn’t always worse. As a result, a neuron won’t be able to capture the pattern.\n- In the Phone Battery example, if students struggle to think about multiple inputs, encourage them to focus on one input at a time. Students can start by thinking about how music affects the battery life, focusing only on cases where video hours is 0. Then, once students have identified the effect of music hours, they can consider video hours.\n- In the Light Switches example, if students struggle to identify if it’s possible or not, ask them to think about the problem in terms of what happens when each switch value is larger or smaller. Help students notice that when the switch values are as small as possible, the output is 0; when the switch values are as large as possible, the output is still 0; and the output is 1 in between.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which neurons were possible to construct? Which neurons were not? What did you notice about the neurons that could not be constructed?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the activity they just completed and consider what they noticed about neurons that were impossible to construct. Students should have been able to correctly find weights and biases for the Cell Service and the Phone Battery examples, but should not have been able to solve the Song Popularity and Light Switches examples. Strong responses will notice either the more complex patterns in the Song Popularity and Light Switches examples or the fact that they involve variables where neither bigger or smaller is always better. In the Song Popularity example, more weeks is better at first, but then it’s worse. In the Light Switches example, turning a switch on increases the output at first, but then it doesn’t when both switches are on. Help students see the similarities between these two examples and the theater seat example from the warm-up."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b818042f-5a60-4223-833e-067aed9d4f21",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e3f122d-e912-44db-bc60-9a2645313ddf",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sometimes, a single neuron, or even a layer of neurons, isn’t flexible enough to learn complicated patterns.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e3f122d-e912-44db-bc60-9a2645313ddf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cc1b05e-1b1b-477b-bb81-533b0d828401",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students train and evaluate a simple single-layer neural network, then assess how well it learned the data’s patterns.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Evaluate a Neural Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cc1b05e-1b1b-477b-bb81-533b0d828401",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13245c2e-c5df-4c25-bc2a-ec98e0222118",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Evaluate a Neural Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13245c2e-c5df-4c25-bc2a-ec98e0222118",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73695edd-fa3a-4f36-85eb-2ecfca43424c",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73695edd-fa3a-4f36-85eb-2ecfca43424c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0bedc9a-1163-41e9-b075-65735844ae0d",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students evaluate a single-layer neural network classifier and identify how well it models a complex pattern.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks students to reflect on what neurons are able to learn.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students as they work through training and evaluating their neural network.\n- Help students identify the columns they need to use for the inputs and outputs of the model. This is a classification model, so students are predicting a particular label: the label column should be the output.\n- Remind students of the library functions to fit and score their machine learning model.\n- Help students interpret the visualization of the model. The individual points are the actual points from the dataset. The shaded background represents the pattern that the neural network learned. Help students see that the neural network learned a simple pattern: it looks like a line where one side is one category and the other side is another category. Then, have students evaluate whether the data itself follows such a simple pattern.\n- If students finish early, encourage them to go back and try the other options that they did not choose. The different choices present different patterns that students can investigate.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an opportunity to evaluate whether students understand the limits of what an individual neuron can learn. If students struggle to identify why their neuron failed to learn the pattern, compare the datasets students are working with in the activity to the problems in the previous activity that were deemed to be unsolvable.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0bedc9a-1163-41e9-b075-65735844ae0d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7703e4ec-2bfd-4969-a5e3-22b3526b618b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e51123f6-c33d-4c6b-ac4f-d620dbc1ed1d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e51123f6-c33d-4c6b-ac4f-d620dbc1ed1d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "48b49bd9-1227-42eb-9e6e-4ac7ef1d6ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c8ed212-28eb-4fa8-8642-64bc97ebc0aa",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we saw several cases where our simple neural networks weren’t able to learn patterns in data. As our data gets more complex, our models need to be flexible enough to learn that complexity. That’s what we’ll explore next time.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c8ed212-28eb-4fa8-8642-64bc97ebc0aa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "48b49bd9-1227-42eb-9e6e-4ac7ef1d6ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28dcacaf-826f-40ea-9a35-a34ac0ba4476",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28dcacaf-826f-40ea-9a35-a34ac0ba4476",
        "lesson_activity.key": "48b49bd9-1227-42eb-9e6e-4ac7ef1d6ac2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f2cf659-b21d-467a-b2d7-9dea69ba652b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Present the scenario that students will explore in the lesson. An online video app knows information about a video and a user and needs to make a decision about whether to recommend the video to the user.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Should the app recommend the video to the user?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students explore evidence and draw conclusions based on the information. There isn’t a single right answer, but strong responses will combine multiple pieces of information to analyze the situation. For example, a strong response might notice that if a video is 9 minutes long, but the user’s average watch time is 4 minutes per video, then it might be that the user will find the video to be too long. Another strong response might notice that if a user has watched 7 videos from a creator but only skipped 5, then there’s a good chance that the user likes the creator. The goal is for students to reach insights like “the video is probably too long” or “the user probably likes this creator” that can then be used to answer the recommendation question: help students identify related pieces of information if they struggle.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f2cf659-b21d-467a-b2d7-9dea69ba652b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1b1f73f-d838-4af6-b3fd-667d56c56be7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86c093a3-5268-45b6-81ce-0df0c17b2f61",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Using the information given to us, we were able to answer smaller questions—like “is the video too long?” or “does the user like the creator?”—that we could then use to answer the bigger question. Today, we’ll see how neural networks can learn to do that too.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86c093a3-5268-45b6-81ce-0df0c17b2f61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1b1f73f-d838-4af6-b3fd-667d56c56be7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "64a13588-d3ce-4046-abc7-69f11452fcb4",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "64a13588-d3ce-4046-abc7-69f11452fcb4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1b1f73f-d838-4af6-b3fd-667d56c56be7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a863feb-24cb-4678-9a2d-826350017883",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn about multi-layer neural networks, their structure, and how they make predictions. Students also work through the specific calculations made by a neural network.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students of the structure of the neural networks they’ve seen before. So far, the networks have had one input layer and one output layer.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Introducing Deep Learning"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a863feb-24cb-4678-9a2d-826350017883",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22e48e83-5f2c-4c9a-a11f-3ba021987b2b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This output neuron is trying to answer the question “How much will you like the video?” It tries to answer that question all in one step, calculating based on all of the inputs at once. But sometimes, it’s hard to answer a complex answer in one step. In the last lesson, we saw cases where neural networks like this weren’t able to learn complex patterns.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22e48e83-5f2c-4c9a-a11f-3ba021987b2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1ad3c4b-a7f7-4f08-aa4c-d1207e1545b3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _hidden layer_.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1ad3c4b-a7f7-4f08-aa4c-d1207e1545b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ddb48af7-21fa-428b-b9ac-23da0139478d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To make our neural networks more capable of learning complex patterns, we can add hidden layers. Each unit in the hidden layer can answer some smaller question about the data. Then, we can use those answers to better predict the output.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The name “hidden” refers to the fact that the values in the hidden layer aren’t directly visible to the person using the neural network: users can see the inputs to the network and they can see the outputs, but they don’t directly see the values calculated in between.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ddb48af7-21fa-428b-b9ac-23da0139478d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83327860-2152-4906-ac60-e34e12df73d8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Present to students a neural network with a hidden layer in between the input layer and the output layer. Explain that each of the units in the input layer is connected to each of the units in the hidden layer. Each of the units in the output layer is then connected to each of the units in the hidden layer.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83327860-2152-4906-ac60-e34e12df73d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "857ff7bb-742b-4b74-ad5c-34e66c3f4a84",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Each unit in the hidden layer can be responsible for figuring out a different piece of information that’s useful for producing the final answer. For example, one unit might answer the question “Is the video too long?” Another unit might answer the question “Is this a creator you like?” Then, the output layer can use the answers to those smaller questions to answer the bigger question: “How much will you like the video?”\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "857ff7bb-742b-4b74-ad5c-34e66c3f4a84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e96ac4b-db3e-4430-9f8d-f7129fece38a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the units in the hidden layer work just like other neurons students have seen before. They take a weighted sum of all of their inputs (multiplying each input by its weight) and add a bias. Units in hidden layers also typically have an activation function (like ReLU, which makes sure all outputs are non-negative).\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The questions attached to the hidden layer units on this slide are representative of the kinds of patterns a neural network might learn. In practice, hidden layer units in neural networks will learn whatever patterns are most helpful for predicting the output, and they might not always have a simple English-language interpretation.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e96ac4b-db3e-4430-9f8d-f7129fece38a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b388078f-13c3-4376-b666-54082afc58f6",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define _deep learning_.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that a neural network can have many hidden layers, each with many units. Deep learning is all about constructing large neural networks with many layers and units. This allows the network to learn many different patterns and answer many different smaller questions on the way to predicting the final output."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b388078f-13c3-4376-b666-54082afc58f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c636e6f6-e02c-4b70-b4d0-125701641c02",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "To build AI that can solve challenging problems, computer scientists often build deep neural networks with many layers that can learn very complex patterns.\n\nNow, you’ll have a chance to see how a multi-layer neural network makes predictions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c636e6f6-e02c-4b70-b4d0-125701641c02",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97f4ef2a-ce05-4d88-8c48-f59641ce37b8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Distribute a copy of the Video Score Activity Guide to each pair.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Video Score Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the calculations of a multi-layer neural network, help them with places where they might get stuck:\n- Students might not know which parameters to look at first. Point students towards the structure of the neural network: the hidden layer units are calculated based on the input layer, and the output layer units are calculated based on the hidden layer.\n- If students have forgotten how to calculate a neuron’s output value, work with them to identify the inputs to the neuron and their corresponding weights. In this example, several of the weights are 0: students can therefore ignore the corresponding input.\n- Students might not remember how to apply the ReLU activation function. After students have calculated a value for the hidden units, have them check whether the result is negative or non-negative. If the result is non-negative (in other words, zero or positive), students can leave their result alone. If the result is negative, students should use a value of 0 for the hidden layer unit value.\n- Students might struggle to calculate the output layer. Remind students that since this neural network has a hidden layer, the output layer is calculated based on the values they previously calculated for the hidden layer. Students should not use the input layer values directly when calculating values for the output layer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What did your neural network predict? Do the results make sense?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should reflect on their neural network’s predictions and compare them to their own intuition. Students should have calculated that Video 1 received a higher score than Video 2. Strong responses will identify that this makes intuitive sense: the user usually watches videos for 4 minutes, and Video 2 is much longer than that. Additionally, the user watches more videos from the creator for Video 1 than the creator of Video 2. If students struggle with the intuitive judgements, refer them back to the first page of the activity guide, which explains the interpretation for each of the input values. Help students understand what each input value represents and what that likely means for whether or not a user will like a video.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out to students that in the activity, each hidden unit had a very clear, human-understandable interpretation. Explain to students that, in practice, when neural networks are trained, the hidden units often don’t have an easily-interpretable meaning. The neural network will learn whatever pattern best lets it make predictions, and often that pattern isn’t easy for people to understand or describe."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97f4ef2a-ce05-4d88-8c48-f59641ce37b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb6219a3-3ef1-4990-a491-1100194e5cd3",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students test a neural network and compare it against one that uses a hidden layer. Students experiment with the number of hidden layer units and the number of training iterations to find a setting that makes predictions successfully.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the syntax for training a neural network with hidden layers. Instead of using `SGDClassifier` and `SGDRegressor`—which create neural networks with no hidden layer— students can instead use `MLPClassifier` and `MLPRegressor`.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point out some hyperparameters that can be used to configure a neural network. Students can specify the size of the hidden layer with a tuple that describes the number of hidden layers and how many units are in each layer. In this example, the neural network has one hidden layer with five units. Students can also specify the maximum number of times the neural network will iterate through the data when training: larger values let the neural network train for longer and can improve performance.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Train a Neural Network",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The “MLP” prefix stands for “multi-layer perceptron”, another name for this kind of neural network.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb6219a3-3ef1-4990-a491-1100194e5cd3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1775cc37-b67e-4608-8a54-273a1c2f12ca",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Train a Neural Network"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1775cc37-b67e-4608-8a54-273a1c2f12ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67aa1dac-d4cf-494b-8fd7-7ce86498d7d3",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67aa1dac-d4cf-494b-8fd7-7ce86498d7d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0c2e83e-a8f9-4f3e-914e-c3b01af01209",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1-2.\n- Level 1: Students fit a neural network to data and compare it against one that uses a hidden layer.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check Your Understanding that asks them to identify why hidden layers can improve the performance of neural networks.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students as they work through training their multi-layer neural network.\n- If students struggle to evaluate which model is better, remind them that the goal for the neural network is to minimize the loss function—in this case, the mean squared error.\n- Help students experiment with different hyperparameter values: students will need to try different numbers of hidden units and different maximum iteration counts. If students are seeing high loss values (similar to what they saw in the case with no hidden layer), encourage them to try adding more hidden units and more iterations. scikit-learn will also warn students if their iteration count isn’t high enough.\n- If students see a “convergence warning”, help students interpret what it means. A convergence warning happens when the model went through its maximum number of iterations without finding the best parameter values yet. In this case, advise students to try increasing the number of iterations.\n- If students ask what the additional hidden layer units represent, remind them that the neural network will learn what kinds of patterns to look for in each hidden unit. While it can sometimes be helpful to think of the hidden units as representing “smaller questions” used to answer the bigger question, in practice the smaller questions aren’t always easy to interpret.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 to evaluate whether students understand the reason why multi-layer neural networks are useful. If students struggle here, you can connect this activity to the warm-up where students identified smaller insights that were useful for making a larger prediction. Help students see that units in a hidden layer can serve a similar purpose: they represent smaller patterns that later layers in the neural network can use to make better predictions. You can also connect the lesson to the previous lesson, where students saw patterns that were too complex for a simple neural network to learn. A neural network with hidden layers might be able to learn those patterns more effectively.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0c2e83e-a8f9-4f3e-914e-c3b01af01209",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f1615bf4-fff0-4b51-bba6-87df8da0e725"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1411b3e-9ac4-450a-8336-3182bfb85bde",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1411b3e-9ac4-450a-8336-3182bfb85bde",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b62064f3-7809-49c2-bb05-e40e08b8e20c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a006a8b-7163-42da-93fd-37a1bbcc47ba",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think back to computing the output of a neural network by hand. What kinds of computation—addition, subtraction, multiplication, etc.—did you do the most?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Introduce the lesson by prompting students to realize a neural network—no matter how large it is—requires only a few, specific kinds of computation: mostly multiplication and addition. Strong responses will recall the formula with which students computed a neuron’s activation: they multiplied each input by its weight, then summed the products and the neuron’s bias. If students don’t recall this experience, consider reminding them of it. Once students have identified multiplication and addition as the two most common computations a neural network requires, emphasize that point and preview the lesson’s content."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a006a8b-7163-42da-93fd-37a1bbcc47ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66bd6d69-e1e0-4529-b986-d8347e76ddf2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1dbdaf3c-3e0f-40c2-8cdf-bc50d42bedde",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Neural networks require only a few, specific kinds of computation: mostly multiplication and addition. This is true whether you have a network of 5 neurons or 5 million. Today, we’ll look at the hardware that helps computers perform this kind of computation efficiently, even as neural networks get larger and larger.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1dbdaf3c-3e0f-40c2-8cdf-bc50d42bedde",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66bd6d69-e1e0-4529-b986-d8347e76ddf2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2247db9b-8c00-428f-8c46-20e2ccad26cb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2247db9b-8c00-428f-8c46-20e2ccad26cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66bd6d69-e1e0-4529-b986-d8347e76ddf2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dffdcde7-bbf4-49b3-8517-e3618531e292",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Guide students in discovering that, to run a neural network efficiently, it helps to have a computer capable of parallel processing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to find a group, then collect the materials for the Processing Neural Networks activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to confirm that they have six unique Processing Neural Networks cards.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Processing Neural Networks",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "To reduce the time it may take to prepare materials for this activity, consider placing students in larger groups—such as groups of three."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dffdcde7-bbf4-49b3-8517-e3618531e292",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb5a2347-a903-4951-be2c-5583cb18691b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Processing Neural Networks activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb5a2347-a903-4951-be2c-5583cb18691b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4df6c570-3de1-42d3-a731-bbd198570f4a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In just a moment, you’ll act like computers and decide how to compute the output of the neural network you see on the slides. Your goal is to compute the output as quickly as possible.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If it’s easier for students to see a larger neural network while working, consider coming back to this slide after providing instructions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4df6c570-3de1-42d3-a731-bbd198570f4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97b314af-26f9-4f45-8d3c-3f447e33e192",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Guide students in beginning the Processing Neural Networks activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97b314af-26f9-4f45-8d3c-3f447e33e192",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0785c60b-ee0d-4688-a66f-c40a7337fbdb",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "On the first page of your activity guide, you’ll notice six slots—one for each of your cards. Each slot represents one time period. You can only put one computation in a single time period. You can read time from left to right.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0785c60b-ee0d-4688-a66f-c40a7337fbdb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78aad0dd-3ab3-4661-ab37-0bb2be32cac5",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Illustrate the mechanics of the Activity Guide with an example: making pasta."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78aad0dd-3ab3-4661-ab37-0bb2be32cac5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a76809a-98d9-4c02-bacd-9b72f861757f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s an example from real life. To make pasta, you first need to cook it. Then, you need to heat your sauce. And finally, you need stir the pasta into the sauce.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a76809a-98d9-4c02-bacd-9b72f861757f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b65396c8-ce76-4a34-8f61-770a1dbff0bc",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the first part of the Processing Neural Networks Activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b65396c8-ce76-4a34-8f61-770a1dbff0bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "84cb3d80-8f6e-460f-9636-18b40d68d0f4",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Place your cards in the order required to compute y. Keep in mind that some computations may depend on others. If so, they’ll need to come after the computations they rely on.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "84cb3d80-8f6e-460f-9636-18b40d68d0f4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c33b984-bcd0-42c2-861f-27b0739b23ca",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who place a computation ahead of one it depends on. For example, can’t place \"Compute y\" first, because it depends on the values of h4 and h5. Prompt them to notice these dependencies, and to resolve them.\n- Students who aren’t sure where to start. Help them focus on computations that don’t have any dependencies, placing those first. For example, because x1 and x2 are neurons in the input layer, students can assume their values are known from the beginning.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to turn to the next page of their Activity Guide. Explain the new scenario."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c33b984-bcd0-42c2-861f-27b0739b23ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b032c9a-b98c-465f-b510-34c8997b5680",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Turn to the next page of the Activity Guide. Your task will be the same, but now you can perform up to four computations in one time period by stacking up to four cards. Your goal is to compute y as quickly as possible. How quickly can you do it?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b032c9a-b98c-465f-b510-34c8997b5680",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fcf7f8d3-471f-4d37-a46d-d4b30deea09b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Illustrate the mechanics of the Activity Guide with the same example, making pasta,  now allowing for the possibility that steps could be done in parallel."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fcf7f8d3-471f-4d37-a46d-d4b30deea09b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "801c0024-dcde-41ca-80dc-0fd1a751e190",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s our example from before again. Now, though, notice that it’s possible cook the pasta while heating the sauce—perhaps by using multiple burners. But it’s not possible to stir the pasta into the sauce until those two steps are complete.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "801c0024-dcde-41ca-80dc-0fd1a751e190",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72212fdb-659b-4d14-a8ea-82b11b72f9b5",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the second part of the Processing Neural Networks Activity, now with parallel processing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look (again) for…\n- Students who place a computation ahead of one it depends on. For example, can’t place \"Compute y\" first, because it depends on the values of h4 and h5. Prompt them to notice these dependencies, and to resolve them.\n- Students who think a computation that is above another computation happens first, even if they’re in the same period. Tell students to consider each time period as atomic: that is, everything in that time period happens at the same time.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "The fewest time steps in which students can complete the computation is three—one for each layer after the input layer."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72212fdb-659b-4d14-a8ea-82b11b72f9b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8018537f-873d-4dee-ab39-6bad24cb1717",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Were you able to speed up your computation? If so, why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to realize that many computations in a neural network are independent—that is, they don’t rely on each other. Strong responses will state that they were able to speed up their computation, such that it finished in as few as three time steps. Strong responses will explain that some computations didn’t depend on each other, so they could be completed at the same time. Guide students towards noticing the kinds of calculations in a neural network that have this kind of independence. Most notably, every neuron in a layer is independent of every other neuron."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8018537f-873d-4dee-ab39-6bad24cb1717",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a7c052e-7ddd-4fd1-b742-220cd1b5e09b",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Being able to perform multiple computations at once is really helpful when computing the output of a neural network. That’s because many computations in a neural network don’t rely on each other.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a7c052e-7ddd-4fd1-b742-220cd1b5e09b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68126e80-33e8-4fa2-9131-3d8fad2e0acf",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *parallel processing*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68126e80-33e8-4fa2-9131-3d8fad2e0acf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0cae3e16-dc09-48b7-9282-f69218b8e657",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Parallel processing is critical to powering modern artificial intelligence. You’ve seen small neural networks of ten or so neurons. But modern neural networks are significantly larger—containing many thousands or millions of neurons. Without parallel processing, it would be impossible to compute the outputs of these networks in a reasonable amount of time.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0cae3e16-dc09-48b7-9282-f69218b8e657",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4c627fec-046c-4926-a021-bcf8521db9fc",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Emphasize the key takeaway from the Processing Neural Networks activity."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4c627fec-046c-4926-a021-bcf8521db9fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c243d542-2136-4c19-8157-0ffe893e9077",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "So what’s the point? Well, as you’ve seen, if we want to run neural networks efficiently, we need computers capable of many computations in parallel. Let’s now look at just the kind of hardware that enables computers to do this.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c243d542-2136-4c19-8157-0ffe893e9077",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4966a5f-161f-4a54-a97e-abf4f14e13af",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to CPUs, GPUs, and their cores.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *central processing unit (CPU)*.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Processing Units"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4966a5f-161f-4a54-a97e-abf4f14e13af",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e09c73ee-b987-4a58-a7f2-28ba09b0d4d9",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A central processing unit, or CPU, is hardware that enables a computer to perform a wide range of computations. Every computer or phone has a CPU, so they come in many shapes and sizes. Some are powerful, while others are power efficient. What they have in common is that they’re designed to be flexible. For example, they’re good at running code with many \"branches\"—possibilities for what could happen next.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students, through prior experience, interest, or exposure, may already be familiar with CPUs. Consider inviting these students to share what they know."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e09c73ee-b987-4a58-a7f2-28ba09b0d4d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50a45cc9-74ef-421a-b967-631e5cdb9ce0",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *graphics processing unit (GPU)*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50a45cc9-74ef-421a-b967-631e5cdb9ce0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2b9ca5db-7e7e-47a7-b1b1-fb484e4cc410",
      "position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A graphics processing unit, or GPU, is hardware that enables a computer to perform many simple computations—like addition—at the same time. Unlike CPUs, they aren’t very flexible. Rather, they’re designed to do one thing: lots of simple computations in parallel.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Some students, through prior experience, interest, or exposure, may already be familiar with GPUs. Consider inviting these students to share what they know."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might wonder why this kind of processing unit is called a \"Graphics Processing Unit.\" Historically, GPUs were developed to speed up 2D and 3D graphics, which demand many computations in parallel."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2b9ca5db-7e7e-47a7-b1b1-fb484e4cc410",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9358f523-17c5-4ac0-bdff-57235ea9c81b",
      "position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *core*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9358f523-17c5-4ac0-bdff-57235ea9c81b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "236078a1-6c5d-4f13-beb0-d1620454c838",
      "position": 26,
      "properties": {
        "description": "One of the biggest differences between CPUs and GPUs can be found in their cores. A core is the physical part of a CPU or GPU that actually performs a computation. CPUs have larger, more powerful cores, but only a few: most often, between 2 and 32 of them. GPUs have smaller, less powerful cores, but a very large number of them: often in the thousands.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "236078a1-6c5d-4f13-beb0-d1620454c838",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bbb6d1e-dda6-4afd-b29c-812a68f8830d",
      "position": 27,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students two \"die shots\": pictures of the physical silicon that makes up a processor. Prompt them to infer which one might be a picture of a CPU, and which one might be a picture of a GPU.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look closely. Which do you think is a CPU? A GPU?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Check students’ understanding of one of the key differences between a CPU and a GPU: that a GPU is composed of many smaller cores, while a CPU is composed of a few larger cores. Encourage students to discuss their choices with friends or a partner, to see if they can sway another person to their view. The accuracy of a student’s guess doesn’t matter as much as what their reasoning shows. Strong responses may infer that the picture on the right could be a CPU. Students might notice the repeating pattern within 8 vertical rectangles, 4 on top and 4 on bottom, which could be 8 larger CPU cores. Similarly, strong responses may infer that the picture on the left could be a GPU. Students might notice the pattern of many small, horizontal rectangles, which could be plentiful, smaller GPU cores. Students who choose the wrong picture with the right reasoning (e.g., \"I see lots of tiny cores, so this might be a GPU\"), should be encouraged that they’re thinking along the right lines and that the expectation is not that they can correctly identify what a core looks like on a die shot.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Consider inviting students to come physically closer to where this slide is being displayed, so they can inspect the photos in more detail."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bbb6d1e-dda6-4afd-b29c-812a68f8830d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8868abca-3d4f-40b3-92d5-dda453fa863d",
      "position": 28,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you know about CPUs and GPUs. But which one would you choose to run a neural network? Let’s find out.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8868abca-3d4f-40b3-92d5-dda453fa863d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9070e74a-d7e9-4a5f-b08d-d5426c272402",
      "position": 29,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Processing Units"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9070e74a-d7e9-4a5f-b08d-d5426c272402",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28fc5fe2-3688-4dc2-91c8-ed562aed2daf",
      "position": 30,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28fc5fe2-3688-4dc2-91c8-ed562aed2daf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b7e55124-2338-4b8c-8053-8fb3d09e05d8",
      "position": 31,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students simulate running a neural network on hardware of their choice: a CPU or GPU with variable core counts. They investigate how long the neural network takes to run on each processor.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who try only a few options, then continue. Prompt them to try additional core configurations or to calculate just how many times faster a GPU runs the network than a CPU.\n- Students who could use additional explanation of what they’re seeing in the widget. Point out that they have a large neural network: a network with an input layer of 1,024 neurons, two hidden layers of 2,048 neurons each, and an output layer of one neuron. When they click \"Run,\" they’re seeing the hardware they’ve chosen compute the output of the neural network—much like they did by hand in previous lessons.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students ask about the monetary or environmental costs of running such a model, mention they’ll explore these questions in the following lesson."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an assessment opportunity, checking whether students understand this lesson’s objectives. Students should be able to correctly identify whether a CPU or GPU best fits the workload of a neural network. They should also be able to explain why neural networks benefit from parallel processing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b7e55124-2338-4b8c-8053-8fb3d09e05d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7581cf77-aa7e-410d-891d-69a3a5ce31a5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "00b9fbfc-7e85-413a-9036-02441dde7502",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00b9fbfc-7e85-413a-9036-02441dde7502",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aeb41a1b-9646-4712-b63c-44ee098f6422"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eddf66f7-fc5b-4cbb-aeaa-9a87a9aa97c4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Have you ever used an app or website where you scroll through a list of videos? How do you think the next video is chosen for you?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Activate students’ prior knowledge of the context for this lesson: the computational costs of neural networks that recommend content to users. To begin the discussion, consider naming a few platforms that offer infinitely-scrollable videos: a few are Instagram (\"Reels\") and YouTube (\"Shorts\"), among others. There are no right or wrong answers in this discussion, but strong responses will suggest that future videos are chosen based on the kinds of videos someone has already watched. Prompt students to name a few metrics these platforms might care about: such as the probability that you watch the whole video, the probability you stay on the platform for another minute, or the probability that you \"like\" or \"favorite\" what you see. Make the point that these systems are really about choosing a strong candidate (i.e., a video you’ll like, with high probability) from a list of possible candidates (i.e., a set of possible videos that could be shown to you)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eddf66f7-fc5b-4cbb-aeaa-9a87a9aa97c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6a6cd60-b4e7-4b67-8359-467a2bb12927"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff1998c7-10b2-483d-9bda-b787ac151bc4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Most platforms that offer a feature like this are solving a particular kind of problem: the problem of choosing something you’ll like out of a larger set of things that could be shown to you. Today we’ll explore the models behind this kind of problem—and focus in particular on the costs of running those models. By doing so, we’ll start to understand how to measure the costs of AI more broadly.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff1998c7-10b2-483d-9bda-b787ac151bc4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6a6cd60-b4e7-4b67-8359-467a2bb12927"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42dacf37-8af0-4d00-81e4-3f79bf8a478a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42dacf37-8af0-4d00-81e4-3f79bf8a478a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6a6cd60-b4e7-4b67-8359-467a2bb12927"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a193cd9-1a67-42ba-bd43-1a20ba305f8e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Introduce students to tools that can help them measure the costs of computation. Students learn to use floating-point operations (FLOP) to describe computational work and FLOP per second (FLOP/s) to describe computational power. They also learn to use wattage to describe the energy consumption of hardware that can perform this computational work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to neural network-based recommendation models.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "The Costs of Computation"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a193cd9-1a67-42ba-bd43-1a20ba305f8e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67e56bb4-475c-417b-9370-68368f2317ee",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A recommendation model is a model that aims to choose content a user might like to see. Today, recommendation models are often large neural networks.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You can learn more about neural network-based recommendation models from https://ai.meta.com/blog/dlrm-an-advanced-open-source-deep-learning-recommendation-model/"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67e56bb4-475c-417b-9370-68368f2317ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df9bf2c6-ed5a-4784-90cd-a5799fb9cabd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe how a recommendation model works."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df9bf2c6-ed5a-4784-90cd-a5799fb9cabd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f459d2c9-e73e-4bf5-9b7a-7bee1399c724",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "For example, a recommendation model could take, as input, information about a user and a potential video. It then returns, as output, the probability the user will engage with the video.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f459d2c9-e73e-4bf5-9b7a-7bee1399c724",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7a892d0c-10c7-4a46-8ba9-3b35c4a4d2b9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect knowing the probability a user will engage with a video to the ability to make higher quality recommendations."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7a892d0c-10c7-4a46-8ba9-3b35c4a4d2b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a1175f1-a25f-44a5-8ca0-4ae9957597d1",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If a platform isn’t sure which video—out of some number of possible videos—you might like, they could use a recommendation model for each video, ultimately choosing the one you’re most likely to engage with.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In reality, a recommendation model can be used to find the probability a user might engage with any kind of content—not just videos. In fact, a common use of recommendation models is to determine how likely a user is to click on a potential ad that could be shown to them. If you have additional time in this lesson, consider exploring with students how the notion of what a \"good recommendation\" is can shape a user’s experience on a platform and may not necessarily be in their best interest."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a1175f1-a25f-44a5-8ca0-4ae9957597d1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f988ba7-782e-4fd5-8f04-ec3cc53442fe",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to the central question of today’s lesson: how much might it cost to run a large neural network, like a recommendation model?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Costs of Computation Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *floating-point operation*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f988ba7-782e-4fd5-8f04-ec3cc53442fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86aef71b-d5e9-424a-b8fb-c1076658bf0f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A floating-point operation, or \"FLOP\", is a single arithmetic operation—like multiplication, division, addition, and subtraction—involving floating-point numbers. Recall that a floating-point number is a number with a decimal in it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86aef71b-d5e9-424a-b8fb-c1076658bf0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "765bbe53-aa75-4cb6-bf27-6bf9b70c4df7",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe a FLOP as a unit of computational work."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "765bbe53-aa75-4cb6-bf27-6bf9b70c4df7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "727c0219-c632-40f6-9848-04b4f8b09d44",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A FLOP is a useful unit, because it allows us to describe how much computational work will be required to run a given model.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "There are multiple conventions for writing or saying the plural of \"FLOP\". Some use \"FLOPs\". However, FLOPs can be confused for \"FLOPS\" or \"FLOP/s\"—both representing a rate—floating-point operations *per second*—rather than the plural of FLOP. Another option is to use FLOP as either singular or plural: one could talk of a single FLOP or multiple FLOP. This lesson opts for the latter, to avoid confusion with FLOP/s."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "727c0219-c632-40f6-9848-04b4f8b09d44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b74c4c6-b209-49f3-b49a-efafa5f4b2d3",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete three sections in their Activity Guide: the Computational Work section, the Computational Power section, and the Energy Consumption section.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, look for…\n- Students who aren’t sure how to calculate the number of weights in the recommendation model. Point them to the key information: each neuron in one layer is connected to every neuron in the next layer. So, if there are N neurons in one layer and M neurons in another, there will be N times M connections—and thus N times M weights. Consider drawing a small neuron network to illustrate this point. If students still find the concept difficult, consider providing them this algorithm:\n  - Find every point in the network where neurons are connected: for example, between the Input Layer and Layer 2, between Layer 2 and Layer 3, between Layer 3 and Layer 4, etc.\n  - Multiply the number of neurons in those two connected layers together.\n  - Sum the products you’ve calculated.\n- Students who need help working with the large numbers (e.g., 1,000 TFLOP/s) on their calculators. Introduce them, if they haven’t been already, to how to enter numbers into their calculator using scientific notation.\n- Students who aren’t sure how to approach a given question. Remind them that all of the information they need has been given to them: but it’s up to them to decide how those numbers relate to answer the question they’re being asked. Also consider creating a simpler version of a question, guiding students to the answer to that question, then asking them to apply the same approach to the more complex scenario."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b74c4c6-b209-49f3-b49a-efafa5f4b2d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c485a767-1b29-4fbc-b6d2-ea15b3ac58fe",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you’ve learned about how to measure computational work and its requirements, tinker with various scenarios a company might encounter and how much they might cost.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c485a767-1b29-4fbc-b6d2-ea15b3ac58fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59224818-4402-4c52-9ff2-7dd5984e88d9",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Costs of AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59224818-4402-4c52-9ff2-7dd5984e88d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "971ca787-676b-4ab2-b776-bad0a651e068",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "971ca787-676b-4ab2-b776-bad0a651e068",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34ae02b4-c181-40e8-a93b-8ed774634740",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Levels 1–2.\n- **Level 1:** Students tinker with a widget that allows them to modify the number of concurrent requests to service, the number of candidate videos to consider, and the generation of GPU to purchase. Students see how computational costs are a function of the hardware you choose and the amount of work you need to do.\n- **Level 2:** Students complete a Check Your Understanding.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 as an assessment opportunity, checking whether students understand this lesson’s objectives. Students should consider the tradeoffs in computational cost and recommendation quality, using data they’ve gathered from the widget."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34ae02b4-c181-40e8-a93b-8ed774634740",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b122b6de-99c9-48e0-a478-25bd27383b57",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Suppose you’re in charge of a large video platform. How many candidate videos should you consider each time a user requests a new video? Consider the tradeoffs in computational cost and recommendation quality.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share and justify their responses from the Check Your Understanding. Especially strong responses will suggest that there may be diminishing returns in recommendation quality relative to cost as one increases the number of candidate videos they consider. For example, it’s fairly likely that one could find a reasonably good video among 100 candidate videos, and the potential increase in recommendation quality by considering 1,000 candidate videos may not justify the ten-fold increase in cost. If students don’t arrive at this conclusion themselves, consider guiding them towards it. Pose questions such as: \"Given 100 videos, how likely do you think it is we could find a good video to show you in that set?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If it’s interesting to students, you might consider sharing that one could solve a problem like this by pre-filtering the list of videos one runs through a recommendation model. Rather than assessing a very, very large number of candidate videos that may or may not be ones a user would like, one could choose the best video from a relatively smaller shortlist of videos they think a user will like. To find this shortlist, one could rely on computation that’s less expensive than running a large neural network: for instance, it’s relatively inexpensive to simply find videos from people you’ve watched videos from before."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b122b6de-99c9-48e0-a478-25bd27383b57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6900936-1d10-4979-97b0-b12d25fdd44f",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students situate the hardware requirements they’ve learned about in data centers, exploring the costs of data centers beyond computation and energy.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Data Centers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6900936-1d10-4979-97b0-b12d25fdd44f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba8ac33c-9d0b-4b73-8aad-b845f5eab8cf",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you’ve seen, at the scale large technology companies operate on, they often need hundreds of GPUs to run a single service. If they run many services, they’ll need tens of thousands of GPUs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba8ac33c-9d0b-4b73-8aad-b845f5eab8cf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1a939615-c5d7-41be-ab06-296b64a24d4c",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *data center*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1a939615-c5d7-41be-ab06-296b64a24d4c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2341cfe-c676-428d-b681-94db1dd9c4f6",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "A data center is a building that’s especially designed to house computer equipment such as GPUs. Data centers have been around for a while, storing data, hosting websites, and more. Relatively recently, however, they have shifted in focus to supporting a very large number of GPUs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2341cfe-c676-428d-b681-94db1dd9c4f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2920a168-e3c7-4306-925b-3c0c976f3405",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to prepare for the \"Inside Google’s Data Centers\" video by finding a partner and getting a blank sheet of paper. Instruct students to look for costs of any form they see while they watch the five-minute video.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the clip from the \"Inside Google’s Data Centers\" video (00:00–05:37)"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2920a168-e3c7-4306-925b-3c0c976f3405",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "924ef7c4-b5a9-4ca5-aaac-7505b81274c1",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We’ve talked about computational costs. But what other costs does AI have? Consider what you can see of its impact on people, the environment, and others.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "924ef7c4-b5a9-4ca5-aaac-7505b81274c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de8e98ce-4a8f-4830-ba3d-6039fb27d941",
      "position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What costs—monetary, social, environmental, or other—did you notice?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to share what they noticed from the \"Inside Google’s Data Centers\" video. Responses will vary. Look for students to mention environmental costs, such as the use of large open land. Also look for students to mention energy costs, such as the cost of cooling water, supplying power to GPUs, and more. Be prepared, too, for students to share costs related to people: the cost of jobs that may be lost, the cost of new worker skills to be learned, or the cost of negative public sentiment."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de8e98ce-4a8f-4830-ba3d-6039fb27d941",
        "lesson_activity.key": "84aba9d6-38b4-418c-b991-186d0e2b3559"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d95de00-0308-413b-a409-94c156457723",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d95de00-0308-413b-a409-94c156457723",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d4c601fc-466a-47d7-992a-50eed41fac96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7ec9eba-633f-4d9a-b185-b58348b6ceee",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How could AI predictions help your community?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students brainstorm possible ways that AI predictions, whether via classification or regression, could help a community. Answers will vary, but strong responses will connect predictions to possible applications in a community: for example, students might say that AI could be used to predict how much food people in the community will need so that food pantries have enough stock, to predict road conditions so that communities can make sure roads are maintained, to predict turnout on election day so that poll workers can be assigned, or to predict a tree’s risk of causing damage so that they can be pruned as needed. Many other applications are possibilities too. The goal is for students to brainstorm practical applications of the work they’ve been engaging in over the span of the course so far.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7ec9eba-633f-4d9a-b185-b58348b6ceee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6943a9a4-d831-4cec-baa8-42ddac395740",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Over the next few days, you’ll build an AI program with neural networks in service of a community project.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6943a9a4-d831-4cec-baa8-42ddac395740",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e74022f-06bf-4c59-b938-650eed141852",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Neural Network Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e74022f-06bf-4c59-b938-650eed141852",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36e31b7e-4afc-45f2-8fb8-ca7a909ef4ea",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students choose a problem to work on and a dataset to help them solve it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the project requirements and project rubric.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Choosing a Problem"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36e31b7e-4afc-45f2-8fb8-ca7a909ef4ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eae08e37-86dd-47f8-b3aa-1719939c780b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this project, you’ll pick a community problem and build a neural network to solve it.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eae08e37-86dd-47f8-b3aa-1719939c780b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51d9d302-f0a6-45b5-bd53-ae243394461a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to ask questions about the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Choose a Project step of the Project Planning Guide. Students choose a community project they’re interested in working on, then brainstorm what type of problem it is and what kind of data they might use.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through project selection, help them connect each project to machine learning fundamentals:\n- If students aren’t sure whether their problem is a classification problem or a regression problem, help them focus on the type of prediction that needs to be made. If students are predicting a number, like the number of households at a food pantry or the number of people at a polling place, then the problem is a regression problem. If students are predicting a category, like the condition of a road or the risk level of a tree, then the problem is a classification problem.\n- Help students brainstorm possible inputs and outputs to the neural network. The outputs are mostly provided by the problem description. The inputs are left up to students to brainstorm: invite them to think about what kinds of information would be useful for making a prediction in their chosen scenario.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51d9d302-f0a6-45b5-bd53-ae243394461a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bbc16502-b1ba-41e7-85a5-4d5acfb8b709",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In order to solve this problem with AI, you’ll need some data to work with. So next, you’ll look at some datasets and start preparing data from one of them.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bbc16502-b1ba-41e7-85a5-4d5acfb8b709",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe40cca0-8ba9-4e93-80cf-12a285275d6c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students start work on their projects, choosing a dataset and preparing it for use in a machine learning model.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Working with Datasets"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe40cca0-8ba9-4e93-80cf-12a285275d6c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "221f90ad-a6b1-4e34-9fab-a3cbc3da0469",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Work with Datasets"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "221f90ad-a6b1-4e34-9fab-a3cbc3da0469",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f536596-25b9-4b35-8348-4519f374a5e2",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Levels 1–3.\n- Level 1: Students choose a dataset to work with by evaluating tradeoffs between different options.\n- Level 2: Students start working on their Python program by reading a CSV file and converting it to inputs and outputs.\n- Level 3: Students build and train a machine learning pipeline with a neural network.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on Levels 1–3, look for:\n- Level 1: Some students might not know which dataset to select. There isn’t a wrong choice, but each dataset offers different tradeoffs, and students should consider those tradeoffs when making their choice. Help students see that Dataset A is local and up-to-date: it contains highly relevant data because it comes directly from the community and was collected very recently. Dataset B is also local and contains more data, but comes with some tradeoffs: some of the data is old and could be less relevant due to changes in the community over time, and the dataset contains fewer features. Dataset C contains the most features and is a large dataset, but it’s also the least specific to the community. Students should decide on a dataset and argue for their choice by comparing the tradeoffs and deciding what they feel is most important.\n- Level 2: Students might struggle to know where to begin when presented with an empty Python file. Help students with the syntax for opening a file and for reading the file as a CSV file. Students might also need guidance for constructing their input and output lists. Students have seen similar logic before in previous lessons: students can use either loops or list comprehensions to generate a list of features. Importantly, remind students that each entry in the inputs list should itself be a list containing numerical features.\n- Level 3: Here, students use the function for splitting data into training and test sets and the method for fitting a model that are familiar from the past few units: remind them of the syntax here if they’re unfamiliar. When students are constructing pipelines, help them include both scaling and an appropriate neural network: guide students to identify whether a regression model or a classification model is the right match for their problem.\n- Let students know it’s okay if they don’t complete all three levels today. They’ll have more time to work on the project in the next two days.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f536596-25b9-4b35-8348-4519f374a5e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "701041b6-7dce-4757-b71d-9d013ad53515",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the Day 1 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "701041b6-7dce-4757-b71d-9d013ad53515",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8cf7ac4d-48f9-4910-9043-6c38cb38d0f7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82b6b0d4-3488-4fc0-a773-cad82234d48e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into groups of 2 to 3 and have them discuss the questions with each other, then invite a few to share with the whole class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How could your neural network help your community? In what ways could it go wrong?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on possible applications of their projects and how their work might help their community. They also reflect on possible points of failure. Strong responses will identify benefits that their project’s AI predictions could have in aiding decision-making in the community, but will also point out possible areas of concern. For example, AI could perform poorly on groups underrepresented in training data or could be trained on outdated information. Students might also mention that people could place too much trust in the model’s predictions, let important decisions be made without human review, or use the model in ways that weren’t intended.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82b6b0d4-3488-4fc0-a773-cad82234d48e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6a9080c-ac6b-486a-8e68-55669bdc49c6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, as you work on building your machine learning model, you’ll also consider user concerns about how AI might go wrong and implement strategies to address those concerns.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6a9080c-ac6b-486a-8e68-55669bdc49c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "156728ad-8e70-4f9a-be95-f8ad9a8a8946",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Remind students of strategies for evaluating how well their machine learning models work.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Evaluating Models"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "156728ad-8e70-4f9a-be95-f8ad9a8a8946",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f5c11887-0d27-43ce-96bb-e8179e076915",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a moment, you’ll continue working on your machine learning models. Once you’ve trained them, you’ll want to test how well they work, and then use them to make decisions.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f5c11887-0d27-43ce-96bb-e8179e076915",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44cba8f9-656f-4e73-b22b-2caa98d1b770",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Remind students that scikit-learn comes with some built-in functions to evaluate how well a model performs. The accuracy_score function is useful for classification models: it can evaluate how often the model predicted the correct category. The mean_absolute_error function is useful for regression models: it can evaluate how far off the model’s prediction was from the correct number, on average.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, you can also encourage students to think about other metrics they would use to evaluate models, particularly when it comes to evaluating their performance in specific circumstances. For example, for students working on the tree maintenance project, they might care about the model’s recall for high- or extreme-risk trees.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44cba8f9-656f-4e73-b22b-2caa98d1b770",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b932e22-41bd-4b96-8106-c3a2e68d2040",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students train and evaluate their neural networks, then consider user feedback to decide how they want to use their neural network to take action.\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Training a Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b932e22-41bd-4b96-8106-c3a2e68d2040",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0133a8b8-169e-4fa7-9ff2-6035cc3e37a8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Train a Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0133a8b8-169e-4fa7-9ff2-6035cc3e37a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ea3fca1-c906-4129-8ade-7c5af69ffc98",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Levels 3-7.\n- Level 3: Students build a machine learning pipeline with a neural network and fit their model to the data.\n- Level 4: Students evaluate how well their model performs on the test data.\n- Level 5: Students read and analyze user interviews where they learn how their model’s predictions should translate into actionable decisions and consider the concerns that people in the community might have about the use of AI.\n- Level 6: Students update their program to make a specific decision based on AI’s predicted output.\n- Level 7: Students add at least one safeguard to their program to address user concerns.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on Levels 3-7, look for:\n- Level 3: Help students with the syntax for splitting data, building a machine learning pipeline, and fitting a neural network. You can also refer students to past lessons or to AI Tutor for help with the syntax. If students struggle with how to use `MLPClassifier` or `MLPRegressor`, compare them to other models that students have worked with like `SGDClassifier` and `SGDRegressor`, which have similar patterns for usage.\n- Level 4: Students might find that their model doesn’t perform well, or they might get a warning that their model has not yet converged. Help students adjust the hyperparameters of their model: for example, increasing the maximum number of iterations or changing the number of hidden units.\n- Level 5: Students should read the user feedback carefully before determining how their program should make a decision. Help students understand how to turn a prediction into a specific actionable decision. For regression problems, students might consider how to convert their output number into a decision: for example, in the election day project, students should think about how to convert the estimated wait time into a number of extra polling workers to send, based on user interviews. For classification problems, students should consider what specific action the community needs to take for each of the categories: for example, in the road icing project, if the prediction is that a road will have ice, then a salt truck needs to be sent. Then, help students consider safeguards to help address user concerns: there isn’t a single right answer here, but students might identify cases that should be flagged for human review (for example, particularly low food pantry attendance predictions) or cases that should override the AI’s default behavior (for example, sending extra polling workers to sites after moving). Students don’t need to address all user concerns, but should brainstorm ideas for addressing at least one concern.\n- Level 6: If students struggle to understand the difference between the AI’s prediction and the decision that needs to be made, refer students back to user feedback in Level 5. There, community members describe how particular predictions correspond to particular actions that need to be taken.\n- Level 7: Help students implement strategies for addressing user concerns. In many cases, this will look like additional conditional statements to check for cases that need extra attention, or it might take the form of changes to the decision-making logic.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ea3fca1-c906-4129-8ade-7c5af69ffc98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "711ad858-a379-43e9-b267-11b819655d4b",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prompt students to complete the Day 2 Reflection section in their Project Planning Guide.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "711ad858-a379-43e9-b267-11b819655d4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23b1fee3-0e18-4cd3-89cf-7b5a1e5663c6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41759b1d-fefb-448f-82db-1a4bf0e80904",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into groups of 2 to 3 and have them discuss the questions with each other, then invite a few to share with the whole class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kinds of issues are your users concerned about? How will you address those concerns?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the user concerns that they explored in the previous lesson and brainstorm ways to address concerns. There isn’t a single right answer: the goal is for students to think about how to ensure that AI is used appropriately. Students might brainstorm cases where human review should be involved, or where someone should audit the results of particular decisions. Students might also consider cases where logic-based rules should override the AI’s normal behavior in some situations. If students struggle, take a look at the user feedback together from Level 5 and consider what kinds of logic would address each user’s concern.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41759b1d-fefb-448f-82db-1a4bf0e80904",
        "lesson_activity.key": "834ba9c0-00b6-4ed1-8e17-ed261bbf3d86"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7f9af1d-e637-467f-a12b-e462a6480209",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, as you wrap up your neural network project, you’ll prepare a digital poster to present your work to the community. In that presentation, you’ll consider your users’ concerns and how your project addresses them.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7f9af1d-e637-467f-a12b-e462a6480209",
        "lesson_activity.key": "834ba9c0-00b6-4ed1-8e17-ed261bbf3d86"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22eade01-df2f-4114-a804-07fa0f5ef489",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students train and evaluate their neural networks, then consider user feedback to decide how they want to use their neural network to take action.\n",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Training a Model"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22eade01-df2f-4114-a804-07fa0f5ef489",
        "lesson_activity.key": "834ba9c0-00b6-4ed1-8e17-ed261bbf3d86"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9449dda7-8b17-4897-898f-2d24b0b0d310",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Present Your Work"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9449dda7-8b17-4897-898f-2d24b0b0d310",
