      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9081b1b9-ee11-488d-93e3-d08c2faa679a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Levels 3-8.\n- Level 3: Students build a machine learning pipeline with a neural network and fit their model to the data.\n- Level 4: Students evaluate how well their model performs on the test data.\n- Level 5: Students read and analyze user interviews where they learn how their model’s predictions should translate into actionable decisions and consider the concerns that people in the community might have about the use of AI.\n- Level 6: Students update their program to make a specific decision based on AI’s predicted output.\n- Level 7: Students add at least one safeguard to their program to address user concerns.\n- Level 8: Students create a digital poster presenting their work to the community.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work on Levels 3-7, look for:\n- Level 3: Help students with the syntax for splitting data, building a machine learning pipeline, and fitting a neural network. You can also refer students to past lessons or to AI Tutor for help with the syntax. If students struggle with how to use `MLPClassifier` or `MLPRegressor`, compare them to other models that students have worked with like `SGDClassifier` and `SGDRegressor`, which have similar patterns for usage.\n- Level 4: Students might find that their model doesn’t perform well, or they might get a warning that their model has not yet converged. Help students adjust the hyperparameters of their model: for example, increasing the maximum number of iterations or changing the number of hidden units.\n- Level 5: Students should read the user feedback carefully before determining how their program should make a decision. Help students understand how to turn a prediction into a specific actionable decision. For regression problems, students might consider how to convert their output number into a decision: for example, in the election day project, students should think about how to convert the estimated wait time into a number of extra polling workers to send, based on user interviews. For classification problems, students should consider what specific action the community needs to take for each of the categories: for example, in the road icing project, if the prediction is that a road will have ice, then a salt truck needs to be sent. Then, help students consider safeguards to help address user concerns: there isn’t a single right answer here, but students might identify cases that should be flagged for human review (for example, particularly low food pantry attendance predictions) or cases that should override the AI’s default behavior (for example, sending extra polling workers to sites after moving). Students don’t need to address all user concerns, but should brainstorm ideas for addressing at least one concern.\n- Level 6: If students struggle to understand the difference between the AI’s prediction and the decision that needs to be made, refer students back to user feedback in Level 5. There, community members describe how particular predictions correspond to particular actions that need to be taken.\n- Level 7: Help students implement strategies for addressing user concerns. In many cases, this will look like additional conditional statements to check for cases that need extra attention, or it might take the form of changes to the decision-making logic.\n- Level 8: Help students reflect on the decisions they made in the design of their program. Encourage students to write about the decisions they made and why they made those particular decisions, especially around the data they chose, the decision-making logic they implemented, and the user concerns they addressed.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students don’t have access to Google Slides, students could also create their posters using another digital tool, or create posters by hand on a sheet of paper.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9081b1b9-ee11-488d-93e3-d08c2faa679a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "834ba9c0-00b6-4ed1-8e17-ed261bbf3d86"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19a1feb5-568a-4763-bb8e-79e8de4a2c7b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students into groups of 3 to 4.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Invite students to open their project digital poster. Have students take turns presenting their work with each other. Invite other students to provide feedback on what they notice and appreciate in each student’s work."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19a1feb5-568a-4763-bb8e-79e8de4a2c7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "834ba9c0-00b6-4ed1-8e17-ed261bbf3d86"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5258b370-8190-474f-b14b-7bcb1d8ddff7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
